At the end of exchanges, the Prague Stock Exchange fell into the Negative
After the fall of Tuesday morning, the Prague Stock Exchange rescinded its losses.
Translating with equities amounted to almost half of the usual daily exchanges.
On Tuesday, from the beginning of the exchanges, the Prague Stock Exchange resumed its fall the day before, when it lost almost six per cent.
This time, the decline is due to the fall in Wall Street equities.
The reaction of the market to the vote in the United States House of Representatives’ vote, which refused to adopt the stabilization plan for the American financial sector, can also be seen.
Loss of equities in Asia
Asian stock exchanges experienced a dramatic fall on Tuesday, even though clues succeeded, over the course of the day, in clearing some of the losses.
The Hong Kong Stock Exchange Hang Seng lost four per cent during trade, but later it erased some of the losses and reduced the fall to nearly 2.5 per cent.
The Hang Seng China Enterprises index following the Hong Kong Stock Exchange movement lost 3.8 per cent, with markets closed in Shanghai.
Sydney Stock Exchange shares lost more than five per cent, but in the long run we managed to reduce losses to 4.3 per cent.
According to its index, the Taiwan Stock Exchange lost 3.6 per cent.
'Histitation about rescuing in the United States will affect financial markets all over the world,' noted Joseph Yam, the head of Hong Kong's monetary office.
Although Hong Kong is part of China, it establishes its monetary policy itself, i.e. independently of the Chinese Central Bank.
Interest rates in Hong Kong have the same level as in the United States.
According to Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd, US legislators should quickly resume the negotiations and adopt the proposal to support the financial system.
Alternatively, other countries might be affected.
The Bloodbath of U.S. Stock Exchanges
On Monday, the U.S. House of Representatives rejected the plan to support the financial system, from which it should have spent up to USD 700 billion (almost 12 bilions).
Legislators refute President George Bush’s appeal in support.
According to Bush, the project should address the main causes of the financial crisis and help to stabilize the entire economy.
American stocks experienced a bloodbath on Monday, and the main equity indices fell the largest in 20 years.
The Dow Jones index lost nearly seven per cent, having fallen from such magnitude for the last time in 1987.
The clue already lost during the vote, but when it became apparent that the proposal had not been adopted, it was in free fall.
Conchaucation ceded: U.S. U.S. Dollars Expelling 700 Million Dollars to Banks
United States congressional leaders and the government of President George W. Bush agreed on a broader form of financial assistance to the U.S. financial system.
It will be put to the vote at the beginning of next week.
U.S. Legislators made a critical turning point in the talks on the adoption of a bailout in the form of financial assistance of 700 Billion Dollars (about 12 trillion kroons), granted to the U.S. financial system.
However, success is not self-evident.
Indeed, before they can publish the final version of the law and put it to the vote, the Congressmen must complete a number of details of the agreement.
The support plan of the financial system will be discussed on Monday in the House of Representatives.
Said on Sunday at Reuters Barney Frank, the Chairman of the House Banking and Financial Regulatory Committee.
According according to sources, on Wednesday as soon as possible, the plan should a priori be put to the vote in the Senate.
According to economists, the announcement that the rescue package will be adopted should be a major primary psychological factor in reviving the financial markets.
Given the complexity of the mechanisms through which market aid can be effective, it will follow "disdecondency".
Paulson: The plan must be effective
"We have made great progress.
We settled our divergnant views on the form of the package of measures for market stabilisation," the Bloomberg democrat Nancy Pelosi told the Bloomberg Agency.
In its view, the final vote could be taken as early as Sunday.
On the night of Saturday to Sunday, legislator representatives met with U.S. U.S. U.S. U.S. U.S. U.S. U.S., U.S. U.S., U.S. U.S., U.S., U.S. U.S.,
It is expected to repurchase unbeatable real-estavities from financial companies that cause considerable losses to financial firms and threaten the stability of the system in its entirety.
'We are in the process of coming to an agreement that will work and that must also be effective on the market.
We still have to pursue this market recovery plan, but I think we are there," said Paulson.
A signal for Asian trade
The global financial crisis has a considerable impact on declining equity markets.
According to the Democratic senator, Harry Reid, legislators make sure that congressional reach a final agreement as early as on Sunday.
For example, they want to defuse investors prior to the start of trade in the Asian financial markets, which are located in the first time zone, where the congressional decision could have an impact on Monday’s exchanges.
For the time being, however, it was impossible to specify with certainty when the two chambers of the United States confesance would vote, or whether the negotiations might not remain stalled, at the last moment, by a particular problem.
Legislators hope that it will be adopted in the coming days.
However, there are still many changes in the law.
The total amount destined for the system should be divided into two parts.
As President George Bush, the first 350-billion-dollar, must be made available as soon as possible.
But if congressional has the impression that the program does not fulfil its function, it has the opportunity to block the allocation of the remaining amount, by following two stages: first an additional USD 100 billion, then, finally, USD 350 billion.
Mr. Bush welcomed progress in the negotiations
Although the president can veto this decision, Congress has an opportunity to dominate it.
Despite these changes, the program’s main idea of obtaining finance for the repurchase of bad real estate securities, whose value fell, because hundreds of hundreds of thousands of Americans are not able to pay their mortgages, was maintained.
'All work is done.
The House of Representatives should be in a position to vote the law on Sunday, and the Senate on Monday," Republican Senator Judd Gregg said.
U.S. President George Bush himself is pleased with the progress in the negotiations.
Spokeswoman Tony Fratto said that Bush was pleased with the progress made on Saturday and appreciated "the bipartisan effort to stabilise our financial markets and protect our economy".
Restriction of gilded parachute
Chief negotiator Chief negotiator Chairman of the Banking Committee of the Senate said that thieves agreed on responsibility for the financial program, the protection of contributors against losses, the adjustment agreement, the progress of the plan over time.
The new law also provides for the limitation of said gilded parachutes for the directors of companies participating in the programme and establishes a committee, which will ensure the supervision of the fund.
The latter should be managed by the Ministry of Finance.
'We worked very hard on the issue and made significant progress towards an agreement that will work and be effective for the market and for all Americans,' said Paulson.
The plan also includes support for homeowners, who have problems to pay.
The government would have to negotiate new down-payment in the case of mortgages that it will redeem, while reducing the monthly payments of debtors and allowing them to keep their homes.
Marek: Psychological assistance
According to Patria Finance Chief Economist David Marek, the proposed plan is a good idea, but its realization is going to be very difficult.
'And this is due to the fact that we don't know what the mechanism and repurchase price of problem-affected assets will be.
This point will be instrumental in deciding on the sharing of losses between the financial sector and the government," Marek told iHNed.cz this week.
Whether the plan will be adopted now, or at the beginning of next week, according to Marek, it is not so important.
'It is much more important for the congressmen to agree, as early as this week, that they support this plan and dedicate it as soon as possible.
This psychological significance of the agreement, providing for the adoption of the plan, matters most at the present time," Marek stressed.
Exterior-threat struggle: MP3 readers against mobiles.
We'll advise you, how to choose
Whereas almost all mobile phones can read MP3 files, no MP3 player can make phone calls.
This seems to be a clear conclusion that it is better to buy only a telephone.
However, there is sufficient reason to obtain an independent reader.
The choice depends entirely on the mode of use and the requirements of the future owner.
It wouldn't be foolish to buy a high-end gramophone for the house to put Maxim Turbulenc's new album.
On the one hand, such musical products do not come out on vinyl disk, and, from the point of view of quality, there is no objective reason for this to happen.
Now, if there's capping vinyl records in your shelves, with art rock, jazz or blues, you're at least probably dreaming of such a gramophone.
The same is true of music during the movement, i.e. compressed music, to put it in a simplified way - music in MP3.
Here too, there are categories according to price and quality.
For one, it may be unacceptable to spend 8,000 kroons for a reader, in the same way that a Pink Floyd fan would find it difficult to withstreat buoyets bought for twenty kroons at the market.
These are two extremities, between which there is enough room for everyone.
The choice is broad.
Battery lifetime is also decisive
Some top-of-the-range music readers are capable of many more things than a simple reading of music, but these are unknown functions, of course, of most mobiles.
No current device is as universal as the phone.
In addition to the main functions, telephone calls and the sending of text messages, it is capable of reading music and films, of recording them, it has a camera of variable quality and can, for example, until it is used for navigation.
And if smartphones and communicators are taken into account, you'll have a small computer in your pocket.
Given that no fridge knows how to grill meat and no television will take photographs, we are witnessing almost a miracle.
However, the universal nature of the mobile is not without drawbacks.
The greater the number of functions that you use, the smaller the interval between recharacters.
And the reading of the music is one of the most important energy goofers.
Freight of mobile devices that are able to read music for ten to twelve o'clock, in most of the cases this figure is much less.
In this respect, music readers (especially the most rudimentary) perform much more efficiently.
In the case of inexpensive mobile phones, expect them to play music for only a few hours.
If, in addition, it happens that you call from time to time, get a second recharacter for the work to be sure - this being valid even for more expensive devices.
Problem will not arise for MP3 readers, which are capable, following recharging, of a continuous operation of at least 9 o'clock, including the lowest performance.
Another problem arises during the recording of music in the mobile.
Sumnautically, a limited part of the devices has a USB universal connector, all the more the more so since not everyone can be directly connected to the computer, without a special software.
You can obviously record music directly on the Memory Card, but few card readers know how to do it without reduction, given the tiny dimensions of the memory cards used in the mobiles.
As far as readers are concerned, you don't need, except for exceptions (iPod), any software.
Simply fill, through the USB connector, the music device coming from the computer.
Most mobiles and readers will themselves create a classified media library according to the interpreter's name, album title, styling, or according to the user's judgement.
Some devices display only an index having a tree structure, like the Windows Explorer, others are capable of both (iriver, Samsung, Sony, Cowon, smartphones with an operating system).
Players are easier to handle, mobiles have more know-how
In the choice of a concrete apparatus, be especially interested in the lifetime of the accumulator, the capacity of the memory, the quality and the type of sound output and, of course, the ease of the control.
If the Music is for you, essentially, a background noise and if in the course of the day you are going to listen that for a limited period of time, e.g. taking the tram, you only need any mobile reading the MP3.
Virtually all mobile phones are capable of this today.
You don't even have to pay too much attention to the music formats that the mobile or the player is able to read.
Everyone manages to read the MP3 and the vast majority of the music that is going to reach you will have precisely this format.
It is one of the most widely used formats, and the vast majority of cameras can also read WMA-format music.
AAC (format used by Apple and its iPods) is less common.
High-end mobile phones also allow, for example, the purchase of music on-line music.
Simply visit, from the mobile telephone, the corresponding web pages of the operator (or those of another supplier) and purchase the song or album in question.
This service on its own should be a persuasive reason for obtaining a musical mobile.
The offer of operators is unfortunately miserable, and in most of the cases it confines itself to fast-moving radio tubes.
Nor are the lowest prices.
Nevertheless, it is generally considered that this way of buying music (whether via GSM, the Internet or even wireless internet) will dominate the music industry in the future.
Most people always put their motives on themselves.
Every time you feel like listening to something, all you have to do is pull the mobile out of your pocket, even though you've forgot your MP3 player at home.
However, it is also important if you still have earphones on you or a hands-free with connection.
If this is not the case, you can use built-in loudspeakers, which practically all mobiles have, but a minimum of MP3 readers.
For many, the main advantage of musical motives is their price.
The cheapest ones normally cost less than three thousand.
For a price of three 4,000 kroons, one can have an already very suitable walkman.
However, in most of the cases, you will be limited by the lack of output for ordinary earphones.
The price of the best and best-to-equipped telephones, also with an entry for ordinary earphones, does not exceed fifteen thousand.
Readers, on the other hand, have the advantage of having an equitable control.
Really, anyone manages to order the most rudimentary readers - which do not necessarily correspond to the cheapest (see our choice).
A minimum of functions; and the resemblance of the control to that of a cassette station, commonly used in the past, or a gramophone, is obvious.
There is no risk of a collision in the form of a mobile internet plugged in by chance, followed by an impure data transmission bill.
Which appliance to choose
Even the cheapest mobiles (e.g. Vodafone 527) generally have a slot for redeemable memory cards (type M2, microSD, and so on).
Even taking into account the additional purchase of such a card, you're not going to exceed two 5,000 kroons.
But be careful.
In principle, the basic models only support maps with a maximum capacity of 2 GB.
Only the dearest ones get away with 8 GB memory cards.
If you already own a mobile and still want to buy an inexpensive MP3 player, take a Cowon, Creative, Sony, Samsung, Emgeton, or iriver.
Depending on capacitance (maximum 2 GB), you do not have to spend more than 1,100 kroons.
If you are one of the most demanding listeners, instead choose specialized mobile-walkman (Sony Ericsson, some Nokia and Samsung).
These apparatuses are provided with independent control keys for MP3, and it is thus that the ease of the control is similar to that of an independent MP3 player.
Sony Ericsson, of which Walkman series mobiles have the same user-saging menu as MP3 readers sold separately, has gone the farthest in this area.
The same service, in the same price category, but with a memory capacity (up to 16 GB) and an accumulator lifetime (normally 15 occupancy, up to 35 o'clock), is provided by an MP3 reader produced by one of the marks mentioned above.
A first virtual operator will win the Cheukemia this very year.
Possibly
The mobile market in the Czech Republic is relatively advanced, offering almost the same services as their partners in Western Europe.
In spite of this, we still lack something - a virtual operator that would force conventional operators to lower prices.
It may not miss it for a long time.
We have already talked on many times on Mobil.cz about the fact that Czech operators are among the most cherished people in Europe.
The Czech Telecommunications Office, which pointed out that the price of an SUSture was higher at home than in the rest of the Union, confirmed this.
For example, following the European Commission’s latest steps in the regulation of roaming charges, there is a risk that paradoxical situation may occur – SMS sent from abroad will for some customers of Czech mobile operators cheaper than in the country.
How is it possible for prices in the Czech Republic to stay so high, in comparison with Europe?
The answer is simple, it lacks a competition that would really want to get into the fight.
This may seem paradoxical, given that we still have three mobile networks and a penetration that far exceeds 100 per cent.
On the contrary, mobile operators like to complain that the Czech market is extremely competitive.
In doing so, Pavel Dvoak, the Chairman of the Council of the Czech Telecommunications Office, who has stated on several occasions that in the Czech Republic there is no room for any other operator chorus with the operators.
It is probably true that a classic operator with its own network apparently no longer has much luck (therefezing now it would only find frequencies difficult).
Now, there is still a possibility - a Virtual Operator.
Virtual operators entered the mobile market had led to a considerable fall in prices, to which conventional operators had to react afterwards.
Yet, even before parties such as Tesco Mobile or Virgin Mobile came into the market, voices similar to those we hear in our home were heard in Great Britain.
However, it became clear that there was still room for such operators and that they could have a real success.
Moreover, we do not have to look too far to find examples.
In the month of February we informed you about the launch in Poland of the Virtual Operator Mobilking.
In just six months, this operator has managed to earn more than 100 000 customers and become the country’s fifth-largest operator.
Let us add that behind the Mobilking, there is the financial group Penta, which, among other things, owns the U:fon in Tchèquie.
The advantage of virtual operators being the possibility of focusing on a much more concrete target group.
For their operation to be efficient, they do indeed need much less customers than a conventional operator.
They are, in fact, exempt from the relatively high construction, operation and maintenance costs of the mobile network.
For example, Virtual operators in around the world are lobbiing as low-cost operators – they offer low-cost calls and text messages, which is often counterbalanced by a limited service offer.
For example, Tesco Mobile in Great Britain offers text messages at least twice as expensive as its competitors.
But, as Mobilking in Poland’s example shows, relying on an unusual target group can also be an advertisement.
Indeed, this operator specifically targets humans.
'Greather men choose Mobilking - an operator that they like and can trust,' says David Seibert, CEO of Mobile Entertainment Company, running this virtual operator.
It is difficult to say if such such a concept could also work at home.
Vodafone Spokeswoman Miroslav zepick, in doubt: "The Czech market is relatively small for a segmental approach, on which most virtual operators are based.
All the more because it is relatively saturated from the point of view of the provision of services."
In the Czech Republic, no lobbies, say mobile operators
Nevertheless, it seems almost certain that if there will be a virtual operator in the Czech Republic, it won't be Mobilking.
'We are discussing the expansion of this successful project, but in the Czecha the negotiations are not very pro-promoting.
The launch of Mobilking will rather take place in other countries in our area," said in a telephone interview for Mobil.cz, spokesperson of the Penta financial group.
It therefore indirectly confirmed that the operators in the Czech Republic were, in any case, not in favour of this idea.
On the contrary, they are trying to do everything possible so that a potential competitor cannot emerge.
The GTS Novera group, which would thus take a new step in order to become Telefonika’s real competitor in the field of corporate customers, is not hiding its intention to launch a virtual mobile operator for quite a long time.
'We attach great importance to being able to offer not only our customers non-Fixed telecommunications, but also mobile telecommunications services.
And the sooner the better.
We have already carried out negotiations concerning this possibility on several occasions with all Czech mobile operators.
With some, we managed to make progress in the negotiations," said Pavel Kaidl Group Spokesman.
So the future may be promising.
According to unofficial information, it seems that we could perhaps welcome the first virtual operator already by the end of the year.
But for the time being, its creation is accompanied by total silence.
'At the moment, we do not believe that there is room on the Czech market for a virtual operator as such.
The Czech market compares with other very specific ones.
For the time being, no subject with a complex and serious project in this area has contacted us,' said Vlastimil Sr'e of Telefonika O2, in his statement for Mobil.cz.
This also relates to the specific nature of the Czech market.
The spokeswowowowowowowowowowowo of T-Mobile Martina Kmelova said in a rare agreement with the O2 statement: 'For the time being, a viable and bilaterally advantageous project, which could serve as a basis.'
She adds: 'It is practically excluded that a virtual operator will appear in the T-Mobile network by the end of the year.
In order to operate in such a limited period of time, we should now have a concrete agreement."
Vodafone instead directs its attention to fulfilling queries to obtain its own 3G license.
'We are open to negotiations with potential virtual operators, and above all in the area of shared costs and infrastructure.
In our view, this means the greatest contribution from a customer point of view.
Vodafone's main strategy in this area is the discovery of a partner to set up a common network for fast mobile internet."
The responsible authorities: in tune with operators
The approach of mobile operators is easily comprehensible, the entry of another operator would mean an increase in competition, would have led to a fall in prices and thus a reduction in their profits.
What is the point of facilitating the emergence of such a competitor?
When it comes to their interests, the operators know that they understand each other in a fair, and the chicany accompanying the creation of the U:fon also bears witness to this.
The greatest apparent pressure was exerted by the company T-Mobile.
This bears witness to the information available to the publicly available from the web pages of the "T".
Nowadays, however, it lends itself as an an argument so as not to accept a virtual operator.
"Competition in the mobile market has recently been amplified by the entry into the market of a Fourth Mobile Operator.
In view of the nature of the RT mobile market, it is clear that the entry of an additional gambler would subject it, and above all its services, to high requirements."
But given the attitude of mobile operators in this context, the indifference of Czech regulatory bodies seems disconcerting.
However, it is not just a question of the attitude of the TA, which, unlike Great Great Britain and other developed countries, in the Czech Republic does not have the power to intervene in economic competition.
It belongs exclusively to the Economic Competence Protection Office.
In the face of this problem, the latter adopts its typical position in the face of this problem.
'The entry of a Virtual Operator into the mobile market would be possible following the conclusion of an agreement between a mobile operator and the Virtual Operator in question regarding leasing the necessary infrastructure.
No such agreement has been reached for the time being, because the offer of the potential parties has a priori not presented sufficient interest for the conventional operators,' said in response in response to our Kristion Chalupa question from the Department of External Relations (OHS).
Such a statement would come as a matter of surprise on the part of operator representatives.
But on the part of an office, which must represent the interests of consumers and, inter alia, also take care that no one takes advantage of its position on the market, such a statement is more than confusing.
All the more so because Chalupa adds: 'Freath abroad where virtual operators are normally present, it can be seen that their entry into the market has contributed to a reduction in the prices of mobile telephone services.'
However, lobbiing an honest functioning of the market and a fall in lower prices does not seem to be part of the interests of the company.
What is indeed shocking is the almost touching concord of the three mobile operators, resulting from their reactions.
The question, if this is indeed a coincidence, arises quite naturally.
By the same to the same way, a gasoline-price exactly the same as all the service stations is probably due to the chance and the hard battle of the competition.
'The Office did not notice any signs of anti-compettive behaviour in this regard, it therefore has no reason to intervene in any way.
None of the operators has a dominant position, therefore none has an increased corresponding responsibility for its behaviour on the market.
The tools needed to open up the mobile market to virtual operators are probably missing even to the telecommunications regulator," said Chaloupa.
Interpreting the dominant position on the market is one of the many points of contention.
Of course, there is an analysis of the relevant markets drawn up by the "T" according to which telecommunications represent, from the point of view of economic competition, a single market.
None of the Czech operators has a dominant position in such a market.
However, some legal experts propose a different view: for the creation of a virtual operator to be possible, the consent of the network operator must always be obtained.
And the mobile operator quite logically has a monopoly on its network.
For example, it necessarily plays a dominant role in a certain segment of its activity.
However, even without admitting this argument, it should at least be worrisome for the OHS that three mobile operators visibly proceed in a similar manner.
However, the complaints of many stakeholders (including GTS Novera already mentioned) show that they face similar constraints in negotiations with all operators.
Relapsing the responsibility for "T" is a fairly common lap in communications, by means of which heaves its hands of the case.
We have already encountered this type of attitude in a number of similar cases.
Unfortunately, there has not been a success in nenovating the recently adopted Telecommunications Act, an increase in the powers of the telecommunications competition in telecommunications, despite the promise of an improvement in the situation.
It must be very well known by the representatives of OHS, but their interests seem to be somewhere else.
When no one's looking, it can work
Having said that, the functioning of a kind of pseudo-virtual operator, several in Czech mobile networks, remains unnoticed.
These are companies that publish their own price lists for mobile telephone services, but mobile telephone services are public only in part.
In most of the cases, there is talk of companies dealing with multi-level marketing.
For example, Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and other mobile telephone services are provided.
The price lists of these firms usually only reach elected officials.
The magic trick consists in the fact that the company does in front of the operator is a classic business customer, which controls a large quantity of SIM cards.
As an important customer, it obtains special price offers (and others).
What is a problem is that the commercial conditions of all Czech mobile operators prohibit such resale.
'While what you are talking about happened, it could be a violation of the contractual conditions that we would examine and which could lead to an end to the collaboration with the company in question,' replied our question about this Martina Kemovic problem.
Nevertheless, it is quite unlikely that an echo of such an activity did not reach the managers of the mobile operators.
In particular in the case of the three companies mentioned, it may be thrifty customers.
Auncun an operator, however, does not act against such a procedure.
In fact, the activities of these companies bring them meaningless benefits and, at the same time, as opposed to genuine virtual operators, they in no way threaten them with their own official marketing.
"The law allows such speculation, however, the services of these companies are used only by a negligible percentage of customers.
In addition, they are obliged to admit that the SIM card in their phone is not registered under their name, so they cannot fully dispose of it, in the same way as ordinary customers of mobile operators."
However, this is precisely what should seem strange to 'T'.
In addition, there is a further question in OHSS.
If mobile operators turn a blind eye to such a procedure, why do they block the creation of a virtual operator?
It must be added that it has relatively broad powers in order to be able to unsubscribe such an activity, and if it does not wish to confine itself to the explanations of the operators, who state to have no idea that this would happen, their contractual conditions prohibiting it.
When we are no longer the dearest country
As we have already mentioned on several occasions, the entry of virtual operators on the mobile market has brought a considerable fall in the price of services.
Virtually in virtually all the countries where virtual operators used to appear, traditional operators were endeavouring, sometimes more and less, to prevent their creation.
The arguments used by the latter were practically the same - a market that was too small or even too saturated, problems with the quality of the services, etc.
It is precisely at this level that the regulatory bodies, which defended not the interests of the mobile operators, but those of their customers took place in the developed countries.
By both formal measures and informal pressure, they ended up obtaining the creation of a first virtual operator, followed by others.
Undoubtedly, the Czech regulatory bodies deal with this issue in the manner of a hot-potato that can be disposed of by transmitting it to a nearby target.
As a result of our experiences, we cannot get rid of the impression that the confusion in skills lies with them in these cases more than profitable.
But don't shudder the sleeve.
If behind-flight information says is true and a virtual operator will soon appear in us, the Czech mobile market may be getting back on the move.
If one of the operators has agreed to the creation of a virtual mobile operator, it will be difficult to remain immutable in the face of rejection (discrimination) by other interested parties.
And we will perhaps stop calling more dearly than in the rest of Europe.
The new anthem Czech has four new versions.
Choose the Best
Listen to the four achievements of the new Czech national anthem.
On Sunday, on Czech Nationality Day, Prime Prime Minister Mirek Topolinek solemnly presented the new records of the national anthem Kde domov maj (Where is my homeland).
The anthem was re-recorded by the National Theatre Orchestra under the structure of the National Theatre.
We've got four versions -- featuring male or feminine solo song, choral performance and an instrumental version.
Singing is provided by Katezina and Adam Plachetka and Adam Plachetka.
The antherities of the anthem are authors of the anthem.
You can listen to all four new versions on the iHNed.cz server.
The Czech national song is regarded as one of the finest hymns.
As he had previously lectured the Czech Public Radio, he chose singers' solo versions of singers with young and representative voices, which are marked and appreciated.
'Since the very beginning, I was trying to take care that the two singers, and the chorus, have a good diction, so that the lyrics can be clearly understood, let them sing simultaneously,' he said.
The Czech national anthem will celebrate its 175th anniversary next year.
In 1834, the song "Kde domov maj resounded for the first time in 1834 on Stavovské divadlo (The Theatre of the States) in Fidlovaika (Kermesse des favotiers), the Czech original piece by Josef Kajetin Tyl, accompanied by the music of Frantyek Okroup.
It has become popular, and people spontaneously appropriate it.
After the creation of Checoslakovic in 1918, the first verse naturally became an integral part of national anthem.
It remained one of the seven National Symbols even after the creation of the independent Czech Republic.
