Sudetens demanded a referendum in the United States
Sudeten Germans in 1945 called for zumava to be integrated into the United States.
This stems from a letter deposited in the collections of the Jiho-esk-v-deck knihovna (Scientific Library of Southern Bohemia).
If we cannot be part of Germany or Austria, we at least want to live under the bosses of the United States of America.
In 1945, some Sudeten German groups allegedly addressed the President of the United States with such a desire.
Among other things, this follows from a letter preserved in a private archive, which is now deposited in the collections of the Jiho-esk-V-deck-Knihovna.
In the letter, the US president did not allow zumavska zupa (forest region south of Bohemia) – annexed by Hitler under the Munich Agreement – to be returned to Czechoslovakia.
'We clearly understood the situation and want to be really sure that the lyrics proclaiming that American troops come to German territory not as oppressors and fenicians, but as liberators,' is written in the letter, signed "People, to whom the fate of zumava is close to".
Given that rallying to Czechoslovakia is allegedly promising - knowing the Czech national character - only brutality, unrelenting attitude, misunderstanding, oppression and bondage, the authors of the letter ask the American President to take into his hands the protectorate of zumava for now and forever.
'We cannot believe, that you would grant this territory to a foreign State to us, which oppresses and destroys the minorities and which in no way contributed to the victory of the Allies without taking into account the opinion of a quarter of a million inhabitants,' is written in the letter.
The so-called reason why the authors are unable to sign the letter is the fear of revengance by "Czech Oppressors".
If the President of the United States doubted the opinion of the Germans, he would only have to organize, according to the authors of the letter, a referendum in the region to establish whether its inhabitants wanted to be part of the United States, or to live under the employers of the United States.
Although the text contained in the letter may, based on current knowledge of post-wolly post-worth history, according to some survivors it illustrates the expectations of Sudeten Germans at the time.
At a time when the Czech Germans must at least give an impression that the question of their expulsion from the republic will arise, very few of them were such a possibility.
Some were waiting until the US and Great Britain were going to achieve some kind of autonomy for the Sudetenland.
Others believed that they would simply remain in Czechoslovakia.
Famous Austrian journalist Gustav Chalupa, who was born in 'eské Budojovice, confirms this.
During the war, he was, as a German of 'eské Budojovice, engaged in navy in the Baltic countries.
When at the end of the war it was captured by the English and placed in the capture camp of the Holstein region, dozens of Czech Germans would come and consult it so that it could teach them the Czech.
'They believed that once they returned to their homes, the Czech would be profitable to them,' said Chalupa.
None of the captured Germans who lived in Czechoslovakia prior to the war considered that, as a result of the Benei Decrees, he would be expelled from the republic.
