Olmert would, in exchange for peace, almost all the occupied territories
Ehud Olmert, the chairman of Israel’s interim government, advocated the restitution of virtually all the territories occupied in 1967, including the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the Golan Height, in exchange for peace with Palestine and Syria.
He made this statement in an interview published on Monday by Yedioth Ahronoth.
'We must reach an agreement with the Palestinians, which means that it will be necessary to return almost every (busy) territory, or even everyone,' says Olmert on a day-to-daily basis.
'We will retain a certain percentage of the territory, but we will have to cemisent to the Palestinians analogous percentage (from Israeli territory), since without that peace will not happen," he added.
He admitted that possible restitution would include East Jerusalem.
"While who desires security in Jerusalem, especially does not want tractors and bulldozers crushing its feet, as happened to one of my best-mafioculators, will have to give up parts of Jerusalem," remarked Olmert, alluding to the two attacks in the city, in July, in which several people were killed and dozens of people were injured.
According to the Prime Minister, it will be necessary to find "a special solution for the Temple Mount, hoscia and historic places" located inside the Old City.
"While who wants to maintain Israeli sovereignty over the entire city will be forced to include 270,000 Arabs.
It won't work," he later stressed.
Retirement from the Golan Height
Olmert was also in support of Israel's retreat from the Golan Heights, which, in his view, was an indispensable condition for the conclusion of a peace agreement with Syria.
This would also require certain efforts on the part of Damascus.
"I am not proposing to conclude the peace with Syria solely on the basis of a Retirement from the Golan," he said.
"Syllians know very well what they're going to have to give up to get the Golan.
They will have to give up their current relations with Iran; they will have to abandon their relationship with the Hiezbollah (Shiite Movement); they will have to give up the constant support that they are attaching to Hamas (Shiite Movement), Al-Kaida (Terrorist network), as well as jihad (holy war) in Iraq," the Prime Minister said.
Questions that arise after the accident: stewards in the because are they illegal?
The law ignores them; Walking in a long-hathriving bus is prohibited
In the context of the Tragic Student Agency coach accident near Padua, Italy, the question arises if stewards and passengers can travel inside the coach.
'While it was to comply with the law in a strict and formal way, the activity of a steward is contrary to the Road Traffic Act,' said the spokeswoman of the Ministry of Transport Karel Hanzelka.
The accident in question, during which a 22-year-old hostess perished, was apparently caused by the driver of a lorry who crossed the path of the coach.
The girl at that time sadly sat next to the driver, in the most dangerous sieve of the vehicle, although she had two other seats at his disposal.
However, the coach can have an accident or even a sudden brake, also at the same time when steward serves coffee or distributes the newspapers.
What's really important is whether the coach is by virtue of its data sheet equipped with upright seats or not.
If this standing seat equipment is uncertified, passengers must remain seated.
Student Agency long-distance buses do not have standing places, which has been confirmed by the director of the company Radim Janura.
'This is an issue that we have dealt with the Ministry and, at the outset, it prohibits any movement of passengers.
Then they annulled it, and we obtained that passengers could go to the toilet during the journey provided they had their own seats," he unveiled.
The law ignores the steward
"There is no unambiguous legal interpretation of steward - no legal requirement deals with this role.
On the other hand, no legal requirements also prohibit the carrier from offering services.
The Road Transport Act is limited to a vehicle receiver; it does not determine its rights and duties," adds the spokeswoman Hanzelka with greater ambiguacity.
On the other hand, the director of the Road Vehicle Traffic Department, Josef Pokorna, believes that the law adequately addresses the issue.
'Equipment depends on whether the coach is authorized to propose standing places in its approval.
"Everything dwells in there," he said.
Coaches are divided into urban racotters, where there are standing spaces and limited maximum, inter-city speed with higher standings and higher allowable speed and long-haundancy coaches with the highest maximum permitted speed and seats equipped with seatbelts.
As in the airplane, says an expert
Jaroslav Hoon, expert at BESIP (G Road Traffic Safety) in safe transport, does not see stewards on-flight movement as a major problem.
"After regular traffic, there is no danger; the situation can be compared with that of service in aircraft.
Here, too, it is not certain when turbulence will occur, and the air hostess can bump his head."
"Now, in the event that the coach had braked abruptly, the laws of physics would of course be valid.
In the same way as in an urban bus or tram.
Carriers must manage steward safety through internal requirements," he said and also felt that from the point of view of the law, the problem had not been resolved.
Janura is ready to abolish these services
Student Agency is the only bus carrier to offer steward services.
He hires two hundred.
Even after this accident, the director does not regard their profession as a high-risk profession.
'Nevertheless, as soon as I have the impression that it is a risky profession, I'll delete it.
Customizers can receive coffee and diary at boarding.
It would also reduce costs,' he says.
The concern for safety is the reason for the lack of stewards in its vehicles, the director of the Asiana Alexey Litvine, who, in comparison with SA, provides a very small number of lines.
"We don't have any hostesses. We've been dealing with them from the very beginning.
Sooner or later it will end in a fatal accident," he said, one year ago, in Toden.cz.
Huge Tragedy Has Arrested
A young hostess from the Plzeo region, who had been working for the company for two years, perished during the accident on the Naples line.
An Slovene lorry probably calmed the emergency shut-off strip without using a blinker, and it blocked the way to the coach.
The latter driver tried to avoid it by means of an abrupt maneuver, but approached him by his right side.
Janura refuses that the steward-rested seats have been segregated.
'In the case of long-distance lines, it is not possible to reserve these seats, they are blocked.'
The company intends to lodge a complaint against the truck driver.
For the compensation of the girl's family, the agency will not wait for the judgement of the court, but in terms of financial compensation it also relies on the culprit.
An amendment to the law?
Not in sight
An amendment of the law, which has been being drafted for more than a year by a group of 30 experts from the Ministry of Transport, could provide details on the movements of stewards on board the coach.
"Now it did not come to any conclusion on this point.
Actually, it is not too interested in buses, but rather in the system of dots, at the maximum speed on the motorway and the use of the laptop at the steering wheel,' says expert Hoon.
'Ministry of Transport doesn't regard stewards' business as something that would fundamentally endanger passenger safety,' says Spokeswoman Hanzelka, pointing out that coach safety control is under the power of the police and occupational safety office.
The hopes of the Dusseldorf are based on Frankfurt.
