The money comes from both private banks and public-executive banks.
In the background, Daughter-Companies Depfa supports losses.
According to a newspaper article, the state financier re-financed in very short-term for long-term projects for which it had borrowed money – which has not been possible because of market distrusions.
In the first half of 2008, the bank's liquidity reserve had been reduced from EUR 95 per cent to EUR 2.37 million; the own capital rate had also slightly weakened from 1.6 to 1.45 per cent.
Ongoing profits and net profits from financial transactions were, compared to the same period in the previous fiscal year, profits from attributing specified receivables and securities as well as the termination of provisions in credit transactions had fallen from more than 70 per cent to EUR 5 million.
It is only thanks to the significant benefits of repaying the downstream accounting costs from the restructuring of the former Depfa group, as well as the redemption of interest according to the book of tax and commercial purchases for Konzern’s companies that the shortfall in earnings of about 16 per cent turned out to be moderate.
After privatization in 1990, the former state financier initially miscocked industrial real-estate loans, which were later filialized at Aareal Bank.
