Sylvana and the Secrets of Box in Bento
When Sylvana Hiltrop, aged 16, has to introduce herself for the first day at Tachibana Highschool, the girls start laughing: German is so "kawaii".
What does that mean?
Sylvana already knows this word thanks to the Mangas - in her new Exchange Log, she talks about Japan.
"The first day of class in Japan.
What I was excited.
Already on the way to go to school scared me.
I live on the outskirts of Tokyo towards Kawasaki and take the 40-minute train to Tachibana Highschool.
I had to find the way by myself, luckily I didn't get lost.
The beginning of prices does not seem to be set as strictly as at home in Lower Saxony.
Pupils appear one after the other from eight o'clock, until nine o'clock minus twenty, a tour de table begins with the teacher, the "home room".
But before the class started, it wasn't for me to introduce myself.
I don't like talking to people at all, especially in front of a class of 40 students.
Since I didn't still have the uniform of the school, I put on an Old Skirt from my host mother, as well as a shirt, blue rising socks and black leather shoes.
"Sayonara She Sylvana... - My name is Sylvana, I've come from Germany, and I've come from Germany and my birthday is a week.
At that time, I was very happy to start learning the language two years ago.
The 31 daughters in the class all made bass masses, cheered with laughter and made signs, and I constantly heard the word "Kawaii."
I already knew him because of my manga.
It belongs to the vocabulary necessary for the survival of Japanese teens and means "cotton, cabbage".
Then I quickly crept into my seat.
The girl beside me gave me a big ironic smile, she thought it was an honor that the correspondent had to sit beside her.
The rest of the hour was quite quiet, but when the gong resonated, I was properly assaulted.
Everybody was talking to who was better, laughed and asked me a lot of questions - in this case, it's quite difficult to understand English Japanese.
During the break, they all pulled out their Bento.
My foster mother had also done this kind of Lunchbox to me - that's really something other than the wretched German snacks.
I had rice with aromatic herbs, small sausages, cucumber salad and algae salad, fruit and a kind of meat surrounded by Tofu.
Most of the time, I don't have any idea what exactly I eat here in Japan.
Things look weird and a completely new taste.
What's certain is that everything is really good.
Okay, except maybe the candied salined plums.
Bentos aren't just lunches in Japanese schools.
Twice it happens that a girl offers a boy a Bento that she has prepared herself, and which is then particularly carefully prepared - in the hope of being able to do a little better the knowledge of the boy during the break.
I've read that in my manga.
I'm curious to know if I could see it here too.
"Why did Japan in particular?" asked my friends when I told them that I wanted to spend a year at school in Tokyo.
Then I always replied:
"Because it fascinates me, what"
At the end of the day, reading manga is what I've enjoyed for years.
In order to get a better understanding of the manga and the culture that exists, at some point, I also started learning Japanese.
For me, it was really clear that I had to travel there one day.
'Cause I'm also faint for traditions, usages and customs.
And right here, they are given a great deal of importance.
On the one hand, Japan is the country of the latest technologies and trends - on the other hand, it is severe, disciplined and traditional.
And as everything is so well organized, I've never felt lost here, even though Tokyo is the largest city I've ever seen."
Rats also have sensation
A rat or cafarian leaked by danger, does that mean that it also feels fear?
Neurophysicist Joseph LeDoux is convinced of this: a lot of animals also have sensations.
But how could we verify that he feels the same thing when he behaves in the same way as we do?
In the online magazine "Edge", contributions have been published for several years by renowned scientists.
SPIEGEL ONLINE presents in a series of articles the responses to the question "What do you expect to be true without being able to have proof of it?"
I don't think animals have sensations and other states of consciousness without me or anyone else having previously been able to prove it just as it cannot be proved that other human beings have a consciousness.
But in that this case, we can at least hope that all human brains have the same configuration.
As soon as we revolve towards other species and address the issues of the feeling or in the general of consciousness, we enter lands that are difficult to pass because Hardware don't match.
When a rat faces a danger, it does the same thing as many other animals: it kills, leaks or attacks.
Human beings behave in the same way, which is why some scientists deduce a comparable subjective experience.
However, I don't believe it.
Two aspects of the structure of the brain make the transposition of our experience subjective to that of animals difficult.
One of the aspects is that circuits almost always associated with the human consciousness include prefrontal lateral Cortex (because of its participation in short-term memory and in leadership and execution functions).
This vast region is much more developed in human beings than in other primates and seems to be entirely absent in other beings.
So at the very least for these aspects of our consciousness that depend on the prefrontal Cortex, including the consciousness of who we are, and the ability to predict and decide, it is reasonable to assume that the human being even distinguishes itself from other primates.
Another significant peculiarity could lie in the linguistic nature of humans: a large part of its existence being linked to language, this often means that its consciousness depends on it.
In this case, the animals could be excluded.
But even if consciousness weren't to depend on language, it would surely influence it, so that animal consciousness, no matter how it is produced, would probably differ a lot from most of our states of consciousness.
This is why it is also difficult to determine what consciousness in other animals may look like.
If this, in-house and subjective, does not allow itself to be measured or, because of this particular Hardware, an analysis on the basis of our own experience, then its observation poses a big problem for us.
Most of my developments refer to the content of conscious experience, but there is another aspect of the consciousness that should be more easily accessible scientifically.
We could look at animal consciousness processes without taking into account their contents, and this is exactly what has happened in primate short-term dem Memory studies, particularly with the example of visualization.
But this approach followed by Christof Koch and Francis Crick, we focus more on the nervous correlations of consciousness than on the causal mechanisms: the two may or may not be the same.
Curiously, this approach also highlights the weight of pre-frontal Cortex in achieving visualisation.
But what then does this mean for e emotions?
Let me conclude that an Emotional system such as that of Fear is activated in a brain aware of its own activity.
So what we're pointing as "shunting" is the mental state in which we find ourselves when the activity of the defense system localized in the brain (or its sequels, like the reactions of the body) grabs grabbing memory.
E e e e e elevations are closely related to the regions of the cortex that characterize primates and especially humans.
Subtle degrees of psychiation, combined with our volubile nature, emerge, because we can, with the help of words and grammar, differentiate and categorize states to implicate them to others than ourselves.
Opposite Antonio Damasio, for example, believes that sensations from primitive activities arise in the Cortex and braintruth-feancing areas, such as Jaak Pankseep which, however, focuses more on the cerebral trunk.
As this network did not change much over the course of human elaborities, it could well be part of transverse sensations of all kinds.
From a purely theoretical point of view, I cannot contradict it, but I do believe that this is inconvenienceable.
Pankseep argues the fact that what appears as fear in humans and rats is probably also perceived as such in both cases: how could we verify whether rats and humans feel the same when they behave in a similar way?
A cockroach leaks in the face of danger, but does it feel fear?
In my view, similar behaviour is not enough to prove identical experiences.
Admittedly, the nervous comparison helps: Rats and humans have similar cerebral trunk trunks, cockroaches on the other hand do not own even a brain.
But is the cerebral trunk at the origin of sensations?
And even if we could prove this for humans: how could we demonstrate it in rats?
I don't think rats and other mammal mammals, maybe cockroaches (knook?) have sensation.
But I wouldn't know how to demonstrate it.
And because their sensations are probably completely different from ours (since human consciousness rests on special circuits and on language), I prefer to explore the emotional behavior than the emotional sensations of rats.
In any case, progress is pognating nervously to the extent that the same can be gauged in rats and in humans.
Since I don't blunt language and consciousness in rats, or sensations, I don't know if they exist.
We could judge this as in the short term, but I'd rather move forward rather than constantly bump myself against a wall.
Indeed, I am a pragmatistic emotion.
