Dark matter influences the theft of space probes
For years, a mysterious phenomenon has left researchers perplexed: dark matter influences the speed of spatial probes during so-called gravitational support maneuvers.
Experts fear that the general theory of relativity will not be completely correct.
For almost 18 years, the "Ulysses" space probe has been flying in space.
As a result of soon-frozen-frozen-frost fuel conductions, cryogenic death threatens it.
For years, a mysterious phenomenon has been a cause of disarray among researchers – the anomaly of gravitational support.
Gravitational support is a maneuver of space probes, through which they gain additional momentum in the gravitational field of a celestial body, the Earth for example.
In this way, probes can save a lot of fuel.
Cassini-Huygens used this physical crawler as well as the Ulysses stove.
Surprising for years, flying objects do not always behave 100 per per cent of the way it is expected according to venerable laws of gravitation.
In addition, irregularities appear in terms of speed.
Probes unexpectedly become quicker or slower.
Until now, physicists could not explain this effect.
They baptized it with gravitational support an anomaly.
Some experts feared that Albert Einstein's general relativity theory, which describes the emptiness force of matter due to the curvature of outer space, may not be entirely correct.
American physicist Stephen Adler of Princeton Institute for Advanced Study continues to believe as iron that Einstein was not mistaken and tries to explain gravitational support an anomaly using the so-called dark matter that astronomers have identified in the universe.
'Darry matter could be responsible for this anomaly, a matter which is also in the vicinity of the Earth,' he writes.
Spacious researchers have only known the existence of dark matter for about ten years.
Apparently emanates a force of emptiness whose effect is observable on other celestial bodies.
On the other hand, it is completely invisible, i.e. it does not send or reflect any electromagnetic radiation, in particular no visible light.
According to the scientists' calculations, dark matter would still represent a good fifth of the entire mass of the universe.
