'There are only a few areas belonging to the city left, and we'd like the city to retain Extra Square," she said.
'They could propose to rehabilitate and manage it, which Avalon could actually do, but we want to keep it public.'
Avalon Bay announced several months ago that it wanted to rejoin the little street and set up seats there, some of which would also be used by a café to which the builder concedes the space.
The proposal for this potential acquisition was made public at this month's meeting.
Michele de Milly, the Spokesperson for Avalon Bay, has stated that although the company wants to operate on the street, this does not necessarily mean that it wants to be the owner.
"Avalon Bay's main interest is to revive this abandoned alley, and make it public," she said, pointing out that it was the city to suggest the sale.
Seth Donlin, a Spokeswoman for Real Necessary Preservation, confirmed that the city wanted to sell the street, further pointing out that the agency's mission was to develop property rather than manage it.
Regardless to its more colourful neighbour, Bowery, Extra Place left a slight historical imprint.
Some believe that Extra Square bears this name since 1802, when Philip Minthorne's large-scale farm legacy was divided, leaving aside this little piece of land, which has since been abandoned.
The rear entrance of the CBGB, a punk rock monument closed in 2006, re-opened at Extra Place; the street is certainly more famous thanks to Ramones' cover photo of Ramones album "Rocket to Russia."
"this place was magnificent," said Danny Fields, the Ramones manager, who took the picture in November 1976.
It was full of splinters, ragstones, barrels, posters, leaf and ropes."
Some residents, including bloggers who had already talked about the move to the luxury of the East Village, have objected to the idea of selling Extra Place, saying that this humble little property should remain public.
Others have proclaimed Extra Squat as a real rarity – a fragment virtually identical to the city that should be plunged into peace.
Jeremiah Moss, who runs a blog called Jeremiah's Vanishing New York (subtitled "The Book of Contestations: A Worm Rejitter Nostalgic on A City in the Extinction Process") reunites the History of the City and Extra Square, published in 1952 in The New Yorker.
Author Brendan Gill wrote: "Extra Place is a narrow little cul-de-sac, dark by day or by night, and bounded by the gates of a rusty iron warehouse and louvers, full of newspapers of the last week flying along the gutters."
With few exceptions, the street always has the same appearance.
A rat shaky runs to the bottom of the alley, close to a door that once led to the CBGB, which has now become the sieve of a fancy garment store.
Graffiti on the walls, an empty 22-ounce bottle of beer rests next to newspapers that have just been discarded.
A few bystanders said they rarely thought about Extra Place, and suggested its development.
Mr. Field, on the other hand, has expressed the view that there are indications of the attractiveness of the street.
"Best years of my life," he said, unfolding in dark, drizzying and disgusting places.
The Bilingual Debate: Immersion in English
In this part of the Education Supervision programme, Bruce Fuller and Lance T. Izumi discuss the position of candidates on bilingual education.
Lance T. Izumi, a former research member in California, and a former director of the Department of Education at the Pacific Research Institute for Public Policy, is the co-author of the book "Not as Good as You Think. Why the middle classes must be able to choose their school."
Attracting support from Hispanic voters is a complicated business.
Barack Obama’s proposals on education are an interesting example of this.
Mr. Obama's campaign points out that "Afif-American and Latino students are less lucciated to graduating than white students." This is a true fact.
In order to combat these differences, Mr. Obama’s education program supports, among so many other topics, “bilingual transitional education,” for those learning English.
However, the question for Obama is whether his desire to carry out a bilingual education that defends the classroom instruction of languages other than English, goes beyond his interest in eliminating the differences.
Take, for example, Sixth Street Prep, an elementary school in the eastern part of Los Angeles County.
The students of this school are mostly Hispanic, and from low incomes.
More than one-by-a-third of the students, made up of many newcomers, study English.
Among CM1 students, 100 per cent passed the 2008 math state exam, an expert level.
Similarly, almost 93% of the students took an English-of-the-art language exam.
This unbelievable success was achieved with an ingrtue other than that advanced by Mr. Obama.
Sixth Street focuses on analysis, practice, a constant assessment of capabilities as well as an exemplary attitude.
In addition, and here is an example that Mr Obama could copy, says Linda Mikels, Principal of the Sixth Street.
Total immersion in the English language focuses on the almost constant use of English in instruction.
Melle Mikels, contrary to bilingual education, told me "we had a huge success with a student who had just arrived from Mexico, 12 months later, if you went without his class, you wouldn't have had any chance to identify him."
'It works,' she observes, 'it has worked for us
Shouldn’t Mr. Obama emphasize the Sixth Street Prep and retaliate in the other schools, the majority of which are Hispanic Students Legislating in English.
The excellent achievements of this school speak for themselves; but policies on education within its party (the Associations for National Education have long supported bilingual education) and its overwhelming support for bilingual education obscure the prognostics of Obama’s response.
Although he was in favour of immigrants learning English, Mr. Obama had recently regarded this issue as trivial, when he proclaimed that we should all stop pollaborating on the wording of English-language laws.
"On the other hand, he said, 'you have to make sure that your child speaks Spanish.
If Obama really wants to eliminate the differences, then it should look carefully at the education models that work, rather than disdain or consider them insignificant.
