Austria is seriously thinking of regaining its courage following its oscillation to the right in yesterday's parliamentary elections.
The two far-right parties that stalled almost 30 per cent of the votes, the Freedom Party and the Alliance for the Future of Austria, conducted an anti-immigrating campaign; some of their slogans were even, according to critics, considered xenophobic.
Heinz-Christian Strache, the leader of the Freedom Party who won more than 18 percent of the vote, led his campaign against wearing headscarves and burkas, and even expressed his opposition to Alaha food.
During his final race in Vienna, he spoke of "a European fraternity" to avoid the rise of Islam.
Both parties are trying to prevent the construction of Mosques and minarets, proclaiming that they would constitute a political symbol of the Islamisation of Austria and Europe.
Polls and the analyses carried out just after the elections defined the far right as the strongest political bloc in the country, which is demonstrated by the majority of young voters who made the difference because they are worried about their future in the European Union.
