"I'm photographing the master out of the stage to capture elements of his character"
No heels, no heels.
The gasoline of the great masters of Flamenco, relieved of all tensions in order to obtain the relaxed appearance of man or woman.
On the one hand the artist.
On the other, the person.
Inseparable except in intimacy, in these unknown moments of the general public, where one discovers in intimate gestures and stolen glances of artists who do not care to go on the stage.
In "Flamenco - people", the theatrical theatrical with singers, dancers and guitarists with this lesser-knocked side of the performers.
In this project, the photographer repeats the particular iconography of flamenco from different angles.
Included in Flemish Photography Month, the exhibition "refleces the international importance of flamenco, bigger every day", explains the creator, which clarifies that "Flamenco - people" "may be anyone but, after having seen the photos, the observer realizes that these aren't typical images of artists on stage.
These "southern-de-strenge" people are artists who are able to pass on stage passion, sadness, joy ... captivating the audience.
To capture personality moments of these performers you don't have to take pictures on stage.
They have a unique personality character.
What I was interested in was to create an intimate atmosphere that would allow me to capture with the came camera the personality moments of flamenco stars."
Along with the "Peopular Chant des Mines" festival, Bauer began a long journey to photograph singers, guitarists or dancing with a plate camera.
These are portraits which, thanks to the choice of the camera and the equipment, recall the classic photography.
The implementation of different stylistic means ruptured with the classic side of the image, giving it a touch of modernity, reflected the perception of flamenco according to Bauer: "Music with very traditional roots that evolves and surprises every day".
It is not da dancing or singing in itself, but the personality and character of the artist captivates the public with its art.
Searching for essentials and magic has been the constant of this work that we present these days at the Galerie El Fotemata.
Flamenco celebrities such as Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales, Vicente Amigo, among others, are part of the selection made from about seventy photographed artists.
The project evolved into an anthology of Flamenco, which will soon be published in a collection of portraits exhibited at the gallery.
The passion for portraits of Bauer led him to rehearse the idea two years ago with the "Portraits of Old athletes".
Although the concepts are different in "Flamenco - people", the photographer admits that he loves to photograph the portrait of people, know them and be able to enter their lives for more or less long.
I don't think in photography portraiture is the most difficult genre.
You find yourself in front of people, characters that sometimes you don't know or who only leave you a few minutes so you can give them a picture.
Despite these circumstances, as a photographer, I'm always trying that my ideas become reality."
This collection of portraits of the current Fllamenco called "Flamenco - people", is exhibited at the Galerie El Fotemata, located at number 20 rue de la Mata (rue parallel to the Peuplerai d'Hercule) which will be opened until 16 October.
Vargas Llosa Confessions The Novel He Writing is "Great Daring"
The novel takes place in places that no one knows and deals with unfamiliar subjects, but once again, Mario Vargas Llosa is determined to advance the new project he is working on, a novel whose main character is Roger Casemant, an Irish nationalist who lived in the Congo for twenty years in the Congo and passed through the Amazon.
The favourite at the Nobel laureate in Literature confessed yesterday at Hay festival Segovia, that the novel currently occupies it "is a real audacity" and that "everything demonstrates that he shouldn't write it".
"If I were reasonable, I wouldn't do it, but I'm going to do it and I'm hoping to finish it," he concluded in his speech at the festival of exchange of ideas and thoughts which for four days saw a hundred writers, historians and journalists and thrifting thousands of people parading in the aqueduct city.
The author of "City and Dogs" was the most awaited figure in the third edition of this festival and fulfilled his contract.
Vargas Llosa, who won the Hay Festival prize by a majority of participants' votes, topped the Juan Bravo Theatre at the Juan Bravo Theatre.
The presenter of the Festival, Félix Romeo, found himself faced with a fascinated public that did not see the past hour during which the Peruvian, with a prodigious memory and with a great dose of humour, retraced the birth of his literary vocation, which is due to "his thirst to read" and "live of new experiences";
"But I don't think literature is a pure amusement, an ephemeral experience," said Vargas Llosa, who said that "has effects" and "make people less alike", because write writers "don't indulging in their goals".
