Isabelle Bayon puts herself in the skin of the Seville dancer Tortola Valencia
A Seville dancing player will pay homage to a sevarian dancer.
The daughter of a Catalan Father Tortola Ferrer and an Andalusian mother (Georgina Valencia Valenzuela), was born in the Triana district and who at the age of three will live in London with her family at the age of three.
She was a free dancing dancing dancing specialist in  oriental dances.
She had an expungeous life, pioneering the liberation of the woman, like Isadora Duncan, Virginia Woolf or Sarah Bernhardt.
She was Buddhist and vegetarian and pleaded for the abolition of the corset that prevented the free movement of women.
The life of this woman who marked the history of da da da dancing in inter-mornating, fascinated Isabelle Bayon.
"I wanted to work with Pepa Gamboa and she offered to interpret the Sevilla dancer Tortola Valencia and it seemed unbelievable to me."
According to the dancer, "We're not trying to reunite her life but to translate her way of thinking.
His dancing headlines reminded me of flamenco.
For example, "butterfly dancing" reminded me of a Cuban popular dancing and snake dancing at some tangos.
Her dancing was sensual, and they told me that my dancing way was the same as her own," she says.
The staggering took place thanks to two remarkable contributions: Miguel Poveda who will again sing Isabela in one of his shows, as he did at "The puerta abierta"; and that of Professor Matilde Coral, who was exceptionally willing to collaborate with her student.
"Matilde transforms Tortola into a mature character.
I'm happily because she agreed to play a role in my work and that honors me."
The staging also took place thanks to the musical composition and guitars of Jesus Torres y Paco Arriaga, the popular song by Michel Ortega, el Pulga y Moi by Moron; the percussion of El Pajaro, as well as the violator by Rafa El Viola and the collaboration of an actor, Juanfra Juarez, "which plays the role of the eternal".
It also recounts, in a certain way, the life of this woman who exhibited himself in such distant places, especially for the time, such as in India, the United States or Russia, but also throughout Europe, since she usually resided in London.
'I wanted to recall her because I think it's these women who have a huge influence in their time and now no one knows or enhances his work,' says Bayon.
The show will start at 8.30 p.m. at the Lope de Vega Theatre
