Microsoft is looking for (virtual) Receiving Receiving
At MIT MIT's Emergical Technologies conference was presented a new system of human image recognition and human interaction, an Artificial Intelligence species with little intelligence but capable of accomplishing a few basic tasks, such as calling a taxi (or in the case of Microsoft, a shuttle shuttle to the company's campus) in response to what he was asked for in a conversation.
Thanks to a camera and a shape and face-recognizing system, this "receptionist" can know how many people there is in the place, who they are - including remembering what positions they occupy - and listen to and hear the key words of their conversations.
But also grouped them according to conversational groups.
In the presentation video, the receptionist detects a third person but understands that she does not belong to the group who is talking to him and that she is not part of the company.
She asks him if she is here to visit someone and she tells him that she'll answer him in a few moments.
All of this without a blue screen.
Impossible.
But, of course, that represents the future.
Now the company has other things to focus on, for example on the next version of its operating system, Windows.
This week we've been talking in addition to a change of strategy at Microsoft.
Windows 7 will be lighter and will no longer have so many associated programs.
Steve Ballmer discovered that if the computers were sold with the necessary instead of unnecessary applications, they would go five times faster.
This represents a discovery since Microsoft, version after version, did not stop adding functions and "extras" to Windows.
The change of strategy is welcome.
Microsoft is right, its operating system loses importance but also its software.
When Google Chrome arrived on the network, I was one of those who embraced the theory that Google Chrome was the first step by Google to compete with Windows.
Several people have rightly stressed that an Internet browser is not an operational system.
The operational system will always be necessary, since it is the indispensable basis for the computer hardware and the software to be understood.
It performs the same function as your wife or husband when you have to say something to your mother-in-law.
It's true.
It will never disappear, but we have to see the "death" of the operational system not as an absolute truth, but as a relative truth.
This week, Ballmer confirmed this idea.
People are paying less and less attention to the operational system, but more and more of the browser.
We turn on the computer only to run Firefox, Explorer or Chrome and every day people spend more time browsing the Internet than browsing windows.
Who do half of the applications provided with the system?
Who can the most can the least.
And Windows 7 will have to contain fewer applications, much less than Vista.
Cheaper, congested month, less complex.
For the time being, Microsoft is more interested in the launch of its own online services to finally become an alternative to Google.
They desperately need it, indeed, and I'll show you an example.
Picture presented in this article, was taken earlier this month in one of the rest rooms reserved for Microsoft employees in Redmond.
On the beverage clerk, a poster asks them to use their own research tool - 'Your research. 'Our future'.
In 2006, a study concluded that 66% of Microsoft employees used Google to search for information on the Internet.
Last week a delay occurred in the new version of Windows Mobile 7.
It won't come out in the beginning of 2009 but towards the end of 2009.
Microsoft will surely attempt to launch Windows 7 and Windows Mobile 7.
The phone, after all, increasingly resembles a computer.
But the launch of Android lagging and the iPhone is already on the market, it will cost them dearly.
