Drunkiel recidivists will have to install 'alcolock'
The Minister of the Interior wants the driver who has had more than three administrative penalties for blood-alcohol, to install in his vehicle a system that prevents the drunken vehicle from being started, or if he does not bleed.
The initiative has already been announced last June and is now reflected in the report of the Minister of State in which this initiative is regarded as a one-year pilot project.
Its aim is the reintegration of the boatmaster by offering road re-rehabilitative courses.
The Minister of State for Road Safety but also the Directorate-General for Road Safety and the Institute of Technology for Automotive Safety will participate in this initiative.
According to statistics, between 30 and 75 per per cent of motorists arrested for driving drunk more than once took the car.
The alcolock would avoid this since it is a system plugged into the vehicle which measures the alcohol level in the breath and prevents the vehicle from starting the vehicle if the driver does not blast before; or when the driver's alcohol content is greater than that permitted.
Its cost is more than EUR 1 000 and is borne by the driver.
In this regard, a study carried out in the United States in 2007 showed that vehicles equipped with this device reduced up to 65 per cent of infringements during the first year of installation.
And in Spain it is estimated that this could save life to 115 people every year.
This measure depends only on administrative sanctions for two reasons: to deal with these behaviours before they are transformed into a criminal offence.
And facilitating its implementation, since the legal reform can be carried out by ordinary channels but, however, the reform carried out by penal means requires a reform of the Organic Law.
The device is not yet commercially available on the Spanish market, although some industrial and functional vehicles already have one on an experimental basis.
Precisely tomorrow, the Congress of Deputies will vote on an initiative of the Spanish Socialist Party which proposes to install in school buses this system as has been done in France.
