1001, the anti-stress number
There aren't just 1000 but 1001 ways to forgive the chaos and frenzy of everyday life.
And to top it all off, they're all listed in a book that will bring relax to booksellers on September 30.
Wath-wakening wrestles us almost when it rings every morning at 7 a.m., but it's even worse when we think about the restless rhythm that current life imposes on us.
The desk is synonymous with tension and back-pain computer.
Stress represents the evil of the twenty-first century.
But there are neither one nor two methods to combat daily fatigue and problems related to the world of work.
But 1001, neither one more nor one less.
Apart from yoga positions, classical massage or 'spa' treatments, Susannah Marriott brings together in the pages of a book a whole 'menu' of methods adapted to the taste and time of everyone.
"1001 Way to Relax '1001 Ways to Relax ' and will arrive at booksellers directly from the publisher Pearson Alhambra and DK.
If someone believes that it is not possible to obtain tranquillity in places such as work or through personal relationships, this 'manual' insecures such a belief.
In addition, this manual offers other methods such as contact with nature or 100% natural recipes that help you relax.
If adrenaline doesn't run in your veins to restore the balance of your body and stress has become a problem, it's time to sit on the couch, disconnect from its mind and focus on reading.
Sleeping
A few children talk during the night.
I don't relate to those waking woken up by recounting to us something that happened the day before, but to the children who talk during their sleep.
The first time it happens it may surprise you, but in reality it doesn't really matter.
It is a benign alteration of sleep that is called somniloquia.
This is quite common, especially among children and adolescents.
Of course, there's more than one who's talking during the night.
This phenomenon can occur very sporadicly (e.g. once in life associated with an episode of high fever) or more periodically (every day, once a week, once a month ...), the day after the child no longer remembers anything.
There is no single cause that determines its occurrence
They are often related to other changes of sleep such as nightmares, nocturnal terrors, nocturnains (paeping in bed) or somnambulism, but this is not always the case.
As we said before, they can also be associated with very high fevers, situations of stress or overexcipitation.
In any other case, it's a little-studied phenomenon.
The intensity with which it occurs is also highly variable.
It may be that it speaks with a quiet little voice, with insulent words, murmurs or, on the contrary, cries, lyrics ...
In the event that somniloquie becomes very common, it would be advisable to observe the child and consult a specialist, to ascertain whether there is a problem that causes a great deal of anxiety and in order to obtain an explanation for this problem.
We've said that it's a benign tweeting, which is why there's no treatment, unless it's related to another altered sleep that can be cured (e.g., if it's related to apnea of sleep or stress).
What can be done is to take into account a number of very simple preventive measures such as:
Try not to excite the child before going to sleep.
As a result, seek quiet games and avoid more or less violent programmes or television series.
With children very nervous who have difficulty relaxing, relaxation techniques can be used to help them subtle.
This will enable the child to sleep more peacefully.
As soon as the somniloquies appear try to stay calm.
Reminder that they are harmless for the child and that the next day he will no longer remember what happened.
Useful advice
Don't kissing him.
It's useless because it's unconscious and you would wake him up.
Even if he's talking, he sleeps and then rests.
Reminder that if he is very anxious several times, it is advisable to consult a doctor or to check what causes so much anxiety in the child.
If you understand what it says (not usual), don't be tempted to query to get information.
You won't get anything, because by dormant, he isn't conscious to organize his thinking.
In addition, you will hear what it says, but you won't know what he's dreaming about, so pay attention to the way in which you interpret the information and the conclusions you draw from it.
Don't Never tell the child that you can interrogate him during the night to know his secrets.
It doesn't even joking.
This causes a lot of anxiety.
In conclusion, don't worry, it's nothing very nasty, it's an episode that lasts just a few minutes.
