Sarkozy to Meet Angrious Fishermen
Before flirting towards the United States, Nicolas Sarkozy will make a detour through Brittany.
Faced with the display of force by the fishermen, who on Monday greatly disturbed the attacks of several Breton and Norman ports, the President of the Republic decided on Tuesday morning to the port du Guilvinec in Finistère to meet their representatives.
They had promised to welcome it "in a constructive spirit" and hoped that it would "come with concrete proposals".
A presidential visit at the same time as a general meeting of 'Chrency committee' created by Breton fishermen to deal with soaring gas oil prices, which reached an average record of more than EUR 1.14 per litre.
According to Elysée Spokesperson David Martinon, the president "discuss with them solutions that can be imagined to help their companies regain competitiveness and to secure the employment and remuneration conditions of seafarers".
Nicolas Sarkozy is thrilled by the fishermen.
On Tuesday morning they conducted snail operations in Morbihan and Calvados, resulting in traffic junkies of nearly 10 km.
Quiberon marina was also shackled, but the connections to Belle-Island not stopped.
A filter dam has taken place on the Quiberon peninsula.
The sailors have already demonstrated their determination on Monday by burning tires and pallets in front of the fuel depots of the ports of Brest and Lorient, while demonstrations were taking place in Concarneau or Saint-Brieuc.
The movement propagated to other ports on the Atlantic and the English Channel, such as Sables d'Olonne in Vendée, where the trawlers stayed dock, or in Le Havre, where about twenty boats blocked the movements of commercial vessels.
Fishermen from La Turballe et du Croisic in Loire-Atlantique stopped in the evening the brilliance of the important refinery Total de Donges.
Boulogne-sur-Mer have decided to wait for Wednesday's ministerial meeting to decide on possible actions.
In Guilvinec, the "Chrency Committee" must indeed decide on Tuesday on the follow-up to the movement and its participation in the planned interview at the Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries in Paris.
But some Breton fishermen do not want to be represented by officials of the National Fisheries Committee, which did not support their movement.
'The strike is going to continue' because 'fishereners won't give up anything' and 'don't want to move to Paris, for example, Liliane Cariou, the chairman of the Lorient Fisheries Committee.
Professional fisherabies, which do not pay taxes on gas oil, are currently paying about 50 cents per litre, but they estimate that the break-even point of a vessel is only with a diesel fuel of less than EUR 0.30 per litre.
