In Minsk, "you noticed that there was an election, you?"
Young people and opponents were demonstrating on Sunday evening in the main square of the Belarusian capital to reject the results of the legislative elections in advance.
Some Hundred Belarusians met on Sunday evening when polling stations were closed in the Central Minsk Square to protest the Fraudulent nature of legislative elections.
Although no results had yet been announced, many young people had come to demonstrate against President Alexander Lukashenko: 'the dictator, resigned'.
Some people admit that they didn't even vote: 'What's the point, since they'll do whatever they want with me,' said a young person.
They do not believe that the regime, which looked good to foreign observers in an attempt to regain language with the European Union, is ready for a democratic turning point.
The sanctions, including the prohibition of visas for 40 Belarusian leaders and the freezing of certain assets, could be lifted if these elections were considered specific by the observers.
According to the opposition.
"We were kept away from the electoral commissions and our observers were unable to take part in comprehension", deplored Yaouri Potemkin, the spokeswoman of the Union of Democratic Forces, a heterogeneous reunion of twelve parties.
"This is a cosmetic change," said Alexander Milinkevich, former opposition candidate of the 2006 presidential candidate and leader of the pro-European movement, "because the important thing is who counts the voices, and at home is the executive power.
Opposition MPs come into the parliament, it is because they will have been appointed, not elected.
But their entry into parliament can mean space on television, meetings in the regions and proposed laws."
The opposition must therefore enter into the political game because 'the dialogue with the European Union must be launched.
If our economy, which is heavily indebted to Russia, comes into a crisis, and we do not reshape each other, we risk becoming a protectorate of Russia.
But we want to be self-suscitivated."
The opposition is concerned that the Organization for Security and Co-Cooperation in Europe following the electoral process will acknowledge the validity of the elections.
She points out that the participation figures seem too high, and the generalisation of advance voting (26% of voters in Minsk) has opened the vote to all the manipulation.
"You have noticed that there has been an election, 'yes' questions the Social Democratic leader Aleksandre Kozulin, one of the last two political prisoners released in August of this year.
There was no one in the polling stations.
And there will be a maximum of five Opposite MPs in Parliament, the ones that are best suited to power."
Silence of Night recedes in the October Square.
President Lukashenko on television and the members of the Central Electoral Commission welcome their success.
Intermediation - Blood returned safe
Héma-Québec celebrated the 10th Anniversary of its founding yesterday, and its president, Dr. Francine Décary, was right to rejoice because Héma-Québec became one of the safest and most effective blood suppliers in the world.
Above all, it regained the confidence of the public that had been seriously shaken by the contaminated blood scandal in the 1980s and 1990s.
As is well known, the national blood reserves managed by the Canadian Red Cross Transfusion Service were contaminated with Acquired Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) and Hepatitis C Virus during the 1980s.
Faced with this drama and the grunt of the public, the Government of Canada established in February 1994 a public inquiry, presided over by Justice Horace Krever, aimed to shed light on the circumstances that had led to such a tragedy and to make recommendations so that such a disaster did not reoccupate.
While Justice Krever was late in tabling his report, some provinces set up a committee with the objective of planning a new blood management system in Canada.
In Quebec, Gélineau committee has fulfilled this mandate.
As a result of Gélineau and Krever reports, two suppliers were created in Canada: Héma-Québec to serve Quebec and the Canadian Blood Society to supply all the other provinces and territories.
"In 1998, when Héma-Québec started its activities, barely 38% of people still had confidence in Quebec's blood system, remembers Dr Décary.
The situation was disgusting.
Driven by efforts to improve the safety of blood products and working in the field during the years that followed, we regained the confidence of the public", she said before proudly stating that in early 2008, nearly 94 per cent of the population in Quebec now had confidence in Héma-Québec.
Dr. Décary, however, keeps in mind that "confidence is something extremely frail".
Since its creation on 28 September 1998, Héma-Québec applied the precautionary principle.
At that time, in Europe there were many cases of the human form of mad cow disease blunder, a new variant of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, recounts the one held strong since its foundation in 1998.
'And we didn't know at all if this disease could pass by blood.
One of the main recommendations of Krever and Gélineau reports was then based, according to the fact that when it is unknowledged and that a serious disease can ensue, in this case Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, which is characterized by degeneracy of the brain inevitably leading to dirt, exclusion measures are introduced.
In April 1999, blood was therefore denied blood of people who had stayed in the United Kingdom for more than a month since 1980.
A few years ago, however, it has been shown beyond any doubt that this disease is transmitted by blood.
This episode thus showed us that the precaluating principle that was applied made sense."
In 1999, a much more appreciable technique was found on the market: nu nucleic acid (TAN) tests which make it possible to directly detect in the blood donors the presence of the virus itself and not that of antibodies against the virus, on which the tests used until then depended.
TANs have shortened the mute period, which lags between the moment when the person was infected and when the first viruses can be detected in the blood by our techniques, the specialist explains.
It was possible to reduce this period to less than one week.
As a result, there has been a substantial reduction in the risk that some donors will be infected unknowingly and that they cannot be detected.
These improvements have led to a significant reduction in the risk of contracting AIDS through a blood transfusion.
Nowadays, every 12 million people run the risk of receiving HIV-contaminated blood.
'In other words, this means that 12 million blood products will have to be dispensed before someone is infected with HIV.
Since Héma-Québec distributes between 400,000 and 500,000 blood products each year, it will take more than 24 years before a case of transmission of HIV by a blood product.
I believe we can now feel comfortable receiving or prescribing a blood transfusion in order to regain its health,' says Héma-Québec's Chief Executive.
'Our challenge for the next decade is to maintain the safety of our products.
We never know which pathogen will emerge in the system.
Just a short time last year appeared on Reunion Island the Chikungunya Virus which, like West Nile Virus (West Nile virus), is transmitted by an insect in the blood.
We need to be constantly vigilant", Dr Décary continued before pointing out that, in addition to a security supply, Héma-Québec must also provide sufficient supplies.
"Treeds are increasing because the population is getting older.
As anti-cancer treatments are more aggressive, patients often need replenishing red blood cells or platelets.
The aging of the population also has an impact on the blood-donor network, which on average only 3 per cent of Quebecers eligible for blood donation."
Any healthy person who is 18 years of age or older who meets the eligibility criteria can, however, give blood every 56 days, i.e. six times a year.
On average, 14 per per cent of people who offered their blood were not accepted because they suffered from AIDS or malaria, for example, and if they had travelled to a malaria-affected country, they would be excluded for a period of up to a period of one year.
'With this 3%, we manage to maintain an inventory that meets the needs of hospitals.
Strict shortages are very rare.
But we need to renovate the members of this donor network as they get older and can no longer don't donate their blood.
It is difficult to convince people to donate their blood, but above all to restrain them and encourage them to become a regular donor.
Today's life is so unbridled, we rarely find the time to spend an hour donating your blood,' Francine Décary saddies.
Héma-Québec with an annual budget of 297 million dollars provides not only blood products, but also human tissues, including bone grafts, heart valves, eye globes and skin that is used almost exclusively for burners.
These tissues are analyzed, processed and frozen before being stored at Héma-Québec which also manages the only public cord blood bank in Quebec in Quebec.
Umbilical cord, which was formerly regarded as biomedical pulp, is replenished by blood rich in stem cells that can be used to treat serious diseases, such as leukemia.
'A few weeks ago, we celebrated the banking of our one-thousand-threat cord blood sample and we're planning to take more than a Thousands a year.
We should start proposing them for gracbreons at the beginning of next year,' says Dr Décary, who describes this cord-blood withdrawal program as very popular.
"Several mothers are disappointed not to be able to have access to it", because Héma-Québec does not have the means to propose this complex and costly program in all hospitals in Quebec.
