Charest based on the North
Prime Minister does not rule out elections before the end of the year
As soon as the General Council of the Liberal Party ended, Jean Charest went yesterday to Jean-Talon in Quebec Quebec, where the new Health Minister, Yves Bolduc (left), is trying to be elected in today's by-election.
Pushing the boundaries of our last main frontier in the north: this is the long-term ambition that Jean Charest presented with visual strength while remaining broadly, at the end of the General Council of Quebec, in Lévis in Lévis yesterday.
Behind him, during his speech, was projected a kind of Moulin with images.
Rotated in a loop, on three huge screens, spectacular squirements of hydroelectric dams, mining, Quebec as seen by "Google Maps", maps, etc.
Major resources as well as during electoral periods.
In the press, moreover, the Prime Minister refused to exclude that he would trigger the elections this autumn, even though he remarked that his schedule was very busy.
"Plan Nord" is the fourth piece of "vision" that the Prime Minister presites for Quebec, that of a "new economic space", which includes an employment agreement with France, an agreement between Canada and Europe and agreements with Ontario.
Frequested in the Prime Minister's Recent Speeches, the opening of the North - the Northern Territory at the 49th Parallel - had never been presented.
However, the Prime Minister merely explained why the North was 'a jewel of immense potential', but did not give any maturity, investment amounts or a specific priority in future projects.
At the most, a few avenues of what could be further developed: in particular hydro-electricity, forestry, but above all the mining sector, boosted by the growth of emerging countries and the reconstruction of public infrastructure.
The North contains "nickel, zinc, copper, platinum, gold, silver, iron, titanium, and even diamond", according enumeration by Mr. Charest.
This "territory twice as big like France", "It's at home, it's in Quebec.
Not only is it at home, but it's in us," he insisted, such as to make it clear that he solicited a national founding myth.
Quebec was built a great deal in its relationship with the North, he pointed out: the Gold Rush in Abitibi, Quebec's engineering, James Bay.
'It's up to us, it's our future," he added, after taking a theatrical pause to contemplate the huge map behind him.
The North, 70 per cent of Quebec's territory, includes a portion of Labrador that Quebec has always claimed, said the Prime Minister at the press briefing.
"This is a traditional position that all Governments have reiterated.
There is a borderline on which there has been no agreement for a very long time, and when the opportunity arises, I reiterate this position on behalf of the government", he said.
On the other hand, "with climate change, we need to rethreach our vision of the North," he said, referring to the North-West crossing, which will open all year round due to melting.
In this new geopolitical context, the Prime Minister believes that occupying the territory is a duty.
What about locals, First Nations and In Inuit?
The Prime Minister stated that the Government of Quebec would pay close attention to them and that the project would benefit young people.
Two former ministers from two different parties, Pierre Corbeil and Michel Létourneau (a former pehetic MP for Ungava), have already started consultations with some 30 communities.
An inter-ministerial committee chaired by Benoît Pelletier and in which Julie Boulet (Transport and Natural Resources) had an important place had begun work.
The Prime-Minister does not rule out a great meeting, 'not necessarily a summit', but a time when 'one give a common vision'.
The development of the North would also be in conformity with the principles of sustainable development.
The Northern Plan will 'conclorted, orderly and respect the environment,' he promised.
Jean Charest described this 'vision' as being in continuity with that of former Liberal Prime Ministers who preceded it.
"Like the liberals before us, we thought differently to turn the challenges into new opportunities for growth and development."
This was also one of the recurrent themes of the weekend, which began on Friday with a large cocktail to mark the 50th anniversary of the arrival of Jean Lesage at the head of the party.
One of the resolutions adopted proposes to re-tname Centrale Beauharnois as an Adélard-Godbout dam.
"From Godbout, who created Hydro-Québec, to Lesage which made the Quiet Revolution and the Nationalization of Hydroelectricity in Bourassa which made James Bay, our party forged the future", said Jean Charest.
In the press point, he invoked another reference, cursed this one, the "Roads to the North" program by John Diefenbaker, Prime Minister from 1957 to 1963.
In approaching the Eastmain1a-Ruper hydro-Electric dam project, Mr. Charest pointed out that its commissioning was planned for 2012.
'It stays between us there, but I'm planning to do it,' he told his activists, cheering applaund in their homes.
Indeed, the Prime Minister, who said in April that there would be no election in 2008 and perhaps not in 2009, did not rule out the possibility of triggering an election this autumn, which the law allows.
He points out, however, that his schedule was fulfilled, notably by a mission of the Federation Council to China and a Quebec-New York summit.
The General Council at the end-of-dance provided the Liberal leader with a platform containing nationalist resolutions, in particular "constitutional aspirations" which have passed as a letter to the post: the PLQ demands in particular the recognition of "Quebec specificity" in the Constitution.
He would also like Quebec to appoint members of the Canadian Radio-television and Telecommunications Commission to have a right of veto over the decisions taken in Quebec.
On the side of the opposition, the broad outlines of the Plan Nord skepticism were greeted with skepticism.
Pèquste Chief, Pauline Marois, emerged from the Developmental Policy of Nord-du-Québec which the Landry government launched in 2001.
In her view, the Liberal government, upon coming to power in 2003, had given it up.
