THE ADQ expressed its support for the adoption of a Northern Plan by the Government.
'But what we want is that this isn't just a show,' commented leader Sébastien Proulx, replacing Mario Dumont, returning from vacation today.
Literature - A dishonoured country
Mr. Vic Toews, former Minister of "Senteity" in Harper's cabinet, allowed herself last summer to tell Louise Arbour that she was "shaking" for Canada.
Throwing the opprobrium upon this woman who has fought an unceasing struggle for the justice of justice and who deserves all our admiration speaks as much about the Prime Minister about Stephen Harper himself.
Here we stand in front of a government which is the only one, among all the Western countries involved, not having repatriated its national locked into the Guantanamo concentration camp.
A Government which takes no account of the fact that Omar Khadr was an all-young teenager at the time of the charges.
The only Government which, despite the fact that Canada had signed the Geneva Convention, denigrated its word and languished in inhumane conditions a child-soldier.
The only person who devolves a parody of justice in order to assess the guilt of the accused.
It should be borne in remembrance that UNICEF, Amnesty International and the Canadian Bar Association, among other things, are pleading for the repatriation of Omar Khadr to receive a fair trial in Canada.
It is this same government that turns a blind eye to torture: it doesn't matter whether a young adult is now psychologically destroyed, probably forever after so many years of abuse.
We do not yet realise this, but at the moment we are witnessing an insidious erosion of our rights, our freedom and our 'democracy', the word that Harper keeps wielding and which sounds so hollow in his mouth.
This is the time when no morality can no longer exist: we can mourn, torture and arbitrarily imprison a Canadian citizen according to the wishes of a minority head of government (what will happen after 14 October...).
Many electors believe that they will find salvation in a party that promotes Virtuousness, Law and Order: they do not see that this government has renounced what made the reputation and the dignity of our country.
Shame, Mr Toews, is there, in this renunciation, and not elsewhere.
Free Opinion - Barrick Gold's Orwellian Speech
In its edition of September 17, Le Devoir published a letter from Patrick J. Garver, Executive Vice-President of Canadian Mining Barrick Gold.
Pursuing an Orwellian approach, he claimed that the world's largest gold company sued six-million-strong libel lawsuit against three writers and a small publisher without the means of launching a "translivable public debate".
While it was true that the judges were independent and impartial, it was not the truth that they judged, especially in the case of libel lawsuits, but the law.
Unsurprudably, this fundamental difference is completely obscured by Mr Garver.
Because Barrick Gold, the best way to conduct a public debate would lead to ruin anyone who dares to support a position that is contrary to his?
Regardless of the outcome of the trial, the authors and publisher of the book Black Canada will have to spend considerable sums of money in order to prepare for their defence and to fight, with very unequal weapons, against an army of lawyers supported by the almost unpaunded pockets of the mine.
Typical in these unequal wrinkles is also often to ruin the defendants in order to impose an amicable settlement that will guggle the opposition in addition to frightening anyone who dare to address the issue again.
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public debate, it was before public opinion that it should have presented its vision of the facts.
Contrary to what is being done in many countries where Barrick Gold works, debates in Canada normally involve the exchange of ideas rather than truncheons.
It was therefore sufficient for the mining industry to open its books, its dossiers and to establish a real debate, through newspapers, the media, publications and the funding of independent research teams that could have shed light on the issue.
But Barrick Gold doesn’t seem to trust the public and dismisses petitions and letters in support of Black Canada’s authors.
Perhaps Mr Garver could explain how he at the same time can ask for a 'public' debate at the same time while disregarding the views of the same audience?
In Vice-President, it seems that this word refers only to greasy-paid lawyers and not all Canadians; this is a rather narrow view of the concept.
In a democracy, even critics have the right to speak.
What Barrick Gold doesn't understand or doesn't want to comprehend is that the public that supports the authors and publishers of Black Canada does so above all because it rejects the blunt way of mining.
This public is prepared to hear Barrick and judging, but on the basis of civilized exchanges of arguments, and not as part of what many perceive as a gaugate prosecution (following the "public debate", Barrick Gold has just laid a formal notice for the authors of Black Canada in urging them to stop the use of this term...).
In addition to Garver’s surreal speech, Barrick Gold’s continuation is a direct attack on freedom of academic research and the quest for truth, which is essential to any democratic society.
It denies, as a bloc, the right to summarital from credible sources and to the debate on facts and interpretations, which represent the very basis of intellectual work.
If it is not possible to study and discuss topics that unpleasant the rich companies of this world in a country such as Canada, on the veritical risk of repeated prosecution, who will be able to do so?
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public and transparent debate, it could do so in accordance with the scientific standards used by the authors of Black Canada.
It has a right of replicability.
The academic world, to which we belonged, has long since managed debates and dissent and resolving conflicts.
Barrick Gold’s disproportionate pursuit makes it clear that, contrary to what it claims, it has no desire for a transparent debate.
This is a loss for the academic world, as well as for public debate in Canada, and it is now time for governments to put in place mechanisms to limit such abusive prosecutions.
Wall Saving Operation – Democrats and Republicans Get Ronked
The Bush administration and congressional leaders, both Republican and Democratic, finally reached an agreement yesterday on the largest economic bailout plan since the Great Depression of the 1930s.
The new USD 700-billion-dollar plan to curb the Wall-Woest Street financial crisis for months has yet to be endorsed by both Houses of Congression.
Among other things: better monitoring by elected officials of its implementation, new mechanisms to ensure the repayment of public money, new arrangements to contain the number of houses seized for non-payment, as well as control over the salaries paid to the managers of rescued companies.
U.S. Treasure Secretary Henry Paulson, House Speaker Nancy Pelosi, and the Republican Representative at the negotiating table, Senator Judd Gregg, announced good news a few minutes after midnight yesterday at the end of a two-during marathon of negotiations.
'We have made great progress towards an agreement that will be effective in the markets,' said Bush administration representative Henry Paulson.
"Everything has been done in such a way as to shield the real economy and ordinary Americans from the current crisis on Wall Street," the democrat said.
'This is the fundamental point: if we don't do that, the trajectory, the chaos and the disruption of Americans' daily lives will be immense,' said Republican Judd Gregg.
Negories had sprinted in the last few hours for fear of further failure on the Investor’s Mood.
The cut-off time agreed was the opening of markets this in the morning.
Associated belatedly with the negotiations, the two candidates in the U.S. presidential election were rather sympathetized to the cartel, but did not fail to express their displeasure that one was forced to arrive at this end.
Democratic Senator Barack-Obsuased the addition of certain provisions to the agreement, such as allowing landlords to keep their homes and on the salaries of state-suppressed corporate executives.
"When taxpayers are asked to take extraordinary measures because of the irresponsibility of some, there is nothing to welcome.
But these measures are necessary," he commented on American television.
Asked about another channel whether he would vote for the proposed plan, its Republican adversary, John McCain, replied: "I'd like to see the details, but I hope so."
'The broad lines I've read that this is something that we're all going to have to digest and we're going to be able to move forward.
The option of doing nothing is simply not acceptable", he said.
The EUR 700 billion plan aims to repurchase the dubious, unbeyoprudent and unleaderable debts of US financial institutions with taxpayers’ money.
It comes after 14 months of a crisis triggered by the bumblebee of a real-estate bubble that initially led to the collapse of sub-prime sub-prime loans before expanding to other more widespread forms of credit securities (PCAAs) and resulting in the freezing of liquidity and the disgress of banks and financial institutions.
It was a group of elected republicans who deflected the first draft of the agreement last week.
It has been believed to have come out of this impasse since the Democrats have agreed that certain “toxic” assets of companies in difficulty should only be insured by the government, rather than purchased.
In order to round-acent angles for everyone in Congress, it had also been added a provision stating that if the government were to incur losses for five years with that programme, it could put in place new taxes aimed at retrieving it.
The new plan provides for the payment of an initial tranche of EUR 250 billion, plus another EUR 100 billion if the President deems it necessary.
The payment of an additional EUR 350 billion would have to be approved by another vote in Congress.
In exchange for its help, D.Wi.W.Wi. will secure equity participation in rescued companies that will pay dividends in the event of a profit that it can resell when the torment passes.
The Federal Government will have the option of delaying the foreclosure procedures concerning the housing attached to home loans covered by this plan, as well as setting limits on remuneration and severance payments paid to the managers of the companies affected.
Investigators of the plan would like to see it put to the vote in the House of Representatives today, and to the vote of the Senate on Wednesday.
Despite their best efforts, it is not yet certain that their plan will have the go-ahead.
Indeed, voices continued against this project yesterday, both in the republican camp and in the Democratic side.
"Customer obviously, Washington doesn't hear you," Republican Scott Garrett, a member of the House's Finance Committee, told his constituent.
"Yes, we have a serious financial problem.
Yes, there are solutions.
But it's not about taking advantage of taxpayers."
Animator of a small group in Congress nicknamed "Skeptic caucus", Democratic Representative Brad Sherman presents things differently, but comes to the same conclusion.
'Feminati eminent professors of economics, including three Nobel laureates, have written to tell us: we are asking the congregation not to hurry and to look carefully at the way,' he said yesterday.
At the same time, in Europe, there had once again been proof of the global nature of the crisis.
At the end of an emergency meeting, the three Bénélux countries announced the partial nationalisation of the Fortis banking group for EUR 11.2 billion (USD 16.3 billion).
The intervention of the governments of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg had been made necessary after the French bank BNP Paribas had indicated that it was abandoning to acquire its competitor in full sprinkling since it lost EUR 2 billion (USD 2.9 billion) in the risk-based mortgage crisis in the United States.
Fortis employs EUR 85 000 and is one of the 20 largest European banks and the ten largest insurers.
'This isn't lost money," said Dutch Finance Minister Wouter Bos yesterday.
In exchange, a voting right is regained in the bank and influence.
This is precisely what sasurans and households now appreciate that the times look unsecure."
Meanwhile, on the other side of the English Channel, the British government seemed to be in the process of nationalising another bank battered by the financial crisis.
According to the British press, the government would take over all outstanding loans from Bradford and Bringley, including more than USD 41 (75.5 billion) of home loans.
The Spanish bank Santander made it known yesterday that it would purchase the British bank's branches and customer accounts.
The Bradford and Bringley would be the second bank, after Northern Rock, which the British government is expected to nationalize since the beginning of the financial crisis.
