The Warsaw Stock Exchange makes a proposal for the take-up of the Prague Stock Exchange
The Warsaw Stock Degree has proposed EUR 200 million, around 5 BKD, for the takeover of 100 per cent of the parcel package from the Prague Stock Exchange.
Ludwik Sobolewski, the Chairman of the Warsaw Stock Exchange, has confirmed this proposal that the Poles have made despite the fact that, previously, the Czechs have excluded the Warsaw Stock Exchange from the circle of potential owners of the Prague Stock Exchange.
The cause of this exclusion is that the Warsaw Stock Exchange is in the hands of the State.
99% of the equities lie in the hands of the Polish State, which wishes to separate more than 60% of its actions within a few months of activities.
According to the Czech economic newspaper E15, the Polish proposal represents the highest offer made so far for the take-over of the Prague Stock Exchange.
According to the Czech press, the Vienna Stock Exchange, the Trans-Atlantic Stock Exchange, the New York Stock Exchange, the London Stock Exchange (London Stock Exchange) and the Deutsche Buerse have so far made an offer.
Petr Koblic, the director of the Prague Stock Exchange, stated last week that the Warsaw Stock Exchange would be the worst new owner, and did not exclude the fact that this could lead to the disappearance of the Prague Stock Exchange in the future.
The value of the Prague Stock Exchange is estimated at between 5 and 10 billion Czech kroons by market specialists.
On the basis of an agreement between the shareholders, some 90% of the documents would be sold.
The largest owner of the Prague Stock Exchange is the Patria Finance Investment Bank with about 25 per cent shareholding, followed by 14.7% from Cesko Sporitelna and Tiger Holding Four S. a.r.l. with 13.5 per cent.
