Smart car imitates bumble feat
What method would be better for developing technologies capable of avoiding an obstacle, other than the imitation of an obstacle detector already existing in nature?
Like, for example, bees.
In the astonishing project presented at CEATEC, taking place in Japan, buzzing bees gave Nissan's initial strategic idea, inspiring them on how to conceive the next generation of obstacle detectors.
In collaboration with the University of Tokyo, Nissan has built a BR23C self-propelled robot car, a BR23C self-propelled control system, which it equipped with bee qualities, the ultimate goal being to encourage the development of a system capable of avoiding any obstacles.
During its robberies, each bee constitutes its own, oval-shaped space, which in fact looks a lot like our secure protective hull - explained at the exhibition, Kazuhhiro Doi, director of the brand's research and development and technological public relations department.
Feeling the faceted-faebee plays an essential role, since it is capable of seeing in all directions, within an angle of 300 degrees.
To imitate face-to-eye functions, the engineers addressed the idea of laser rangefinders.
The latter identifies the obstacles in an angle of 180 degrees and at a distance of 2 meters in front of the BR23C, it evaluates the distances and sends a signal to the on-board microprocessor, which transforms it into an avoidance maneuver immediately.
The perception of the obstacle takes place in a fraction of a second. Then, the mini auto robot, imitating the movements of the bee, immediately changes the direction of its wheels by turning it by an angle sufficient to avoid the collision.
In contrast to any currently existing system, this sieve is proceeding entirely instinctively.
'Whiblging this is not the case, the mini auto robot wouldn't be able to react quickly enough to avoid the obstacle,' explains Toshiyuki Andoh, the director of Mobility Laboratories and the robotic car project.
It must react instinctively and instantaneously, since this technology corresponds to the most vulnerable and in-interiest layer of the protective casing, a layer where for the moment the collision is considered inevitable.
This whole process must reflect what the bee does to avoid other bees.
The process must take place in the blink of an eye.
Opposite bees, the difference cannot go upwards, downwards or diagonal, it can only be of two dimensions, and only in the direction in which the wheels can rotate.
In this way, instead of the infinite combinations of bees to avoid other bees, in our case we could only use the rotary movement, the accelerator as well as the slowdown as maneuvers at the disposal of the mini-booth robot.
BR23C uses lithium-polymer batteries which are lighter and with an energy concentrate which is larger, making their lifetime longer than in the Lithium-ion version.
According to Andoh, instinctive intelligent reaction is paramount, not data processing, let alone the ability to store.
This mechanism should process only a few incoming signals per second, and react to them.
There is no need to store them and to react to intuitive logic based on data from previous maneuvers; in this way, it uses only a few dozen kilobytes of comprehension continuously.
We don't need a giga-byte capacitance, we don't need even mega-bytes.
This is the first step towards the design of instantaneous siencing technology. According to Nissan's estimates, this system will enter mass-produced cars over the next ten years.
Another application could be its inclusion in the GPS satellite navigation system, so that it can guide the driver to its destination by avoiding the obstacles in its path.
