The written version of the 700-billion-dollar cost-relie-and-dollar cost-reserve economic rescuing the banks’ dubious loans is ready.
In the first lap, half of this amount is going to be spent.
Reuters announces that during further debates after midnight on Saturday, local time on the east coast, congressional leaders have agreed to the controversies that have existed for days and days around the bailout plan for a total amount of USD 700 billion.
'We have taken a serious step forward' - said Nancy Pelosi, the Democratic Speaker of the American House of Representatives, who only has to put the agreements on paper.
Compromise, born from the plan, is supported by both presidential candidates, Barack Obama and John McCain.
U.Treafraaster Secretary Henry Paulson has said by commenting on the results of the trade-off that the program is going to work and will be effective, yet other measures still need to be put in place.
The written version of the plan was made public before Asian markets opened on Sunday evening at 8 p.m p.m. local time (i.e. Monday at 2:00 a.m., according to Central European local time), but did not cause a dramatic increase in the stock markets.
On Monday, the House of Reministes will be the first to pass the bill in the name of the Emergency Economic Stabilization of the Year for 2008, after which its fate will be in the hands of the Senate.
On Saturday evening, President George W. Bush spoke to Pelosi, then the White House also greeted the news of the agreement.
"We are pleased with the progress made that night, and we greatly appreciate the efforts of both parties to stabilize our financial markets and to protect our economy" - said Tony Fratto, the White House Spokeswoman.
On the other hand, the program diverges from the Bush administration’s original proposal, which stated that the USD 700 billion had to be expused in just a single operation on the repurchritation of dubious loans.
From then on, a maximum of one-half of USD 1.7 trillion can be expeded; the first USD 250 billion will be issued immediately after the adoption of the law by Congress, probably on Monday, which will be used by the Secretary-General of the Treasurer to buy bad debts from the banks, while the use of an additional 100-billion-dollar will be left to the discretion of the President.
The programme will be supervised by an independent body, in which the President of the Federal Reservation will also take part.
Spending the second half of the USD 700 billion depends on a new congressional decree.
According to the Act, the Treasury Secretariat will be able to put in place a combined strategy: at the same time, it may, at the same time, purchase bad debts, titles relating to dubious mortgages and miscarriages, as well as other “toxic” types of “potential” claims.
Competent persons of the Ministry have stated that redemptions may take place as part of an auction, but also directly.
The rule provides an opportunity for the companies concerned to take part in an insurance programme, in which the treasury secretariat guarantees the tools that give rise to the worries, for which the companies will have paid a premium equivalent to foreseeable damage.
Henry Paulson has stated that the law provides the necessary tools to dedicate USD 700 billion for the urgent needs of the financial system, whether it be the widespread repurchase of dubious debts, its insurance, or avoiding the risk that the system will lead to the collapsing of any major financial institution.
'We are going to take the necessary steps to be ready for the application of the law, as soon as it is signed' - he added.
On the other hand, bank executives in poor posture and waiting for state aid can expect their severance payments, commonly referred to as gilded parachute across-the-Atlantic, will be captivated by the government.
Wage clipping is mentioned only in very specific cases, in addition, the limitations depend on the manner in which the treasury secretariat offers assistance to the company.
The principle of capping means that if the treasury secretariat re-purchases as part of an auction, they will levy a tax surplus on gilded parachute, unless the discredited bank president retiring.
Directly following the repurchase of bad debts, the government will intervene aggressively to ensure that the amount paid to executives is not exaggerated.
In order for the image to be complete, it must nevertheless be added that the existing gilded parachutes are not modified; the capping applies only to new contracts, thereby motivating participation in the plan.
This is the first time in the history of the United States that legal clippings will be in force regarding the directors-general’s salary-for-term adjustments – commented the Chairman of the Committee for Financial Benefits in the Chamber of Deputies, Democrat Barney Frank.
In addition, the State obtains a right to acquire shares in banks taking part, in this way the risk of the taxpayers decreases, since they can even win in the case as soon as the economy and with it the banks saved fall back on their feet.
The bill mentions that, over the next five years, the President must submit to Congress a proposal that will decide on the way in which the losses will be returned to the taxpayers from the financial sector.
On both sides, of course, there were those who expressed their doubts about this plan.
It's not why the U.S. citizens mandated us, this doesn't protect taxpayers' dollars -- said California Republican MP Darrell Issa.
His Ohio-Demorial colleague, Mary Kaptur, believes that it is the same people who point to taking action, as those responsible for the collapse of Wall Street.
Industrial prices continued to rise
In August 2008, domestic industrial sales prices increased by 0.1 per cent compared to the previous month and by 12.0 per cent compared to the month of August 2007, the Central Statistical Office of Hungary.
Industrial export sales prices increased by 1.1 per cent compared to July 2008, compared to a year earlier, compared to year-on-year-old prices, they decreased by 3.9 per cent.
As a result of fluctuations in domestic and export prices, industrial producer prices rose by 0.7 per cent compared with those of the previous month, and by 3.2 per cent compared to the previous year.
Domestic industrial sales prices rose only to a lesser extent: 0.1 per cent compared to the previous month.
The domestic sales prices in the processing industries have increased only slightly by 0.1 per cent compared to July.
In August 2008, the most significant price increase was observed in the lea Leather and shoe manufacturing sector (2.8 per per cent), with the manufacture of saucepans and bed bellies with a monthly increase of 4.6 per cent, followed by shoe production with 2.5 per per cent monthly increase.
The level of higher prices of plastic and chemical raw materials resulted in a considerable monthly increase in the prices of the manufacturing sector for products and chemical materials (1.6 per cent).
Deflation resulted in the oil-transformation (1.4 per cent), machinery and electrical appliance industries (0.9 per cent), as well as a minimal increase in the wood-processing sector (0.1 per cent).
Prices of paper production, publishing and printing have not changed.
Other sectors in the processing industry increased between 0.2 per cent and 0.9 per cent.
Prices rose by 0.3 per cent in August, mainly due to the effect of the increase in the price of gas, prices rose by 0.3 per cent in August, mainly as a result of the increase in the price of gas introduced by the authorities since 1 July.
In a 12-month comparison, taking into account the domestic sale of the processing sectors, it was the prices of the oil-processing sector that rose most significantly (32.4 per cent).
Metallurgical manufacturing had seen a 13.2 per cent increase in prices, while prices rose by 11 per per cent, while prices had risen by 11 per cent.
Decreased prices have taken place in the production of textiles and textile products (2.5 per cent), as well as in the manufacture of machinery and electrical appliances (0.0 per cent).
In the other sectors of the processing industry, the increase in prices varied between 0.6% and 9.1 per cent.
In the processing industry as a whole, prices increased by 10.2 per cent over the past year.
The prices of the electricity, gas, vapour and water supply sectors were 18.7 per cent higher than in the previous year.
Among the domestic trade groups and depending on the earmarking of sectors, in August it was the initiators of profits on investment that showed the most significant monthly price increase (0.7 per cent).
Prices increased by 0.3 per per cent in the sectors producing consumer goods, while prices in energy-producing and materials for further processing did not change compared to the previous month.
In August 2008, compared to the same month of the previous year, the largest increase could be observed in the energy and material production sectors for further processing (16.8 per cent).
In the production sectors of consumer goods, an inflation of 6.8 per cent has been measured, while for profit-bearing sectors has fallen by 1.4 per cent.
Export sales prices in the HUF industry increased by 1.1 per cent in August than in the previous month, up from 3.9 per cent over a 12-month decline of 3.9 per cent.
In one month, our national means of payment was weakened by 1.7 per cent against the euro and by 7.1 per cent against the United States dollar; compared in August 2007, increased by 7.5 per cent against the euro and by 15.9 per cent against the dollar.
