The European Summit for Equal Opportunities has begun in Paris in Paris, hosted by France as a representative of the current Presidency of the European Union.
In its message to the plenary assembly, as one of the main spokespersons for the expert consultation, the Fidesz European People's People's Party (Fidesz MEP) drew attention to segregations in education, because it is a violation of the law that most affects Roma children and, as a result, one of the main obstacles to their success in life.
On behalf of the French Presidency, this high-level conference was opened by Xavier Bertrand, French Minister of Labour and Social Affairs, by Vladimir Spidla, European Commissioner for Labour, Social Affairs and Equal Opportunities.
Those invited to take part in the consultation include the European social partners, as well as the most eminent representatives of legal protection and European civil bodies, in the field of equal opportunities.
According to the Press Office of the Group of Fidesz Parti Populaire, he warned that, in addition to the distinctions made during recruitment, the Roma disappeared from the labour market, in the first place, as a result of their low qualifications.
'Anwrunprovical majority of young Roma do not have secondary graduating proficiency, as a result of poor basic education, only a very small party has the opportunity to graduate diploma."
At the same time, he had stressed the fact that education as an elementary human right played a key role in asserting other fundamental human rights.
Investing in the early education of Roma children means an increase in the economies of the Member States of the European Union.
According to the MEP, it is necessary to raise the awareness of these relations in the broadest possible circle, also between Roma and non-Roma.
The sooner a Roma child began school, the more he increased his chances of becoming a productive member of the labour market; through the taxes paid on the basis of his or her income and consumption, he participated in the State budget.
"From their citizenship of the European Union, the Roma must be assured of their right to quality education, and be protected from low-quality and sectarian education."
According to the honourable Member, cooperation by the European Union oriented towards an objective and an action is necessary for an immediate acceleration in the process of desegregation of schools, for which purpose, compliance with existing anti-discrimination directives in preparation is of paramount importance.
'We need to break the vicious circle of poverty, segregation and non-utilget, Roma children must be given the opportunities guaranteed by democracy, so that they can participate as full citizens in the future of Europe' - demanded Jurika.
On behalf of the "European Year for Equal Opportunity for Equal Opportunities for All", the aim of the European Equal Opportunities Summit held every year since the summit in Berlin last year is to ensure a unique platform to support the measures taken for equal opportunities and the fight against discrimination, targeted at European and Member State level.
