No Opposition MP acceded to the Belorussian parliament after Sunday's elections.
According to the Russian observers, this can be explained by a country led by Aleksandr Lukashenko, which ensures its prosperity and stability, while according to the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE), this is explained by illegality committed during the counting of votes.
The United Nations Office on Foreign Tourism Organization (OSCE), having dispatched 450 observers from 43 countries, did not recognize the elections.
Although the 110-strong legislature in this country of 10 million inhabitants has been going very well in opposition for many decades, ahead of yesterday's elections, the Opposition uniting its forces as the United Nations Democratic Forces (ODS) strongly hopes that Lukashenko, renewing his mandate as president for the fourth time in dubious elections, in the footsteps of the modification of the constitution in 2006, 'perceiled' the presence.
In this way, ODS has abandoned its originally planned co-clication.
The fact that Belarus, an increasingly economically dependent on Russia, would like to improve its relations with the EU, where because of the Belarusian political order described as an anti-democratic, Lukashenko and several high-ranking politicians did not receive an entry visa, was one of the expectations of the opposition.
The fact that the OSCE, in the evaluation of the campaign, focused on positive changes rather than on shortcomings, such as the prohibition of one-third of opposition candidates, referred to prior appeasement.
However, Monday's OSCE communiqué was already contesting the campaign, during which the majority of opposition candidates could not appear in the media, and in particular the fact that the opposition could not be present during the counting of votes.
In spite of all this, the opposition still feels a palpable improvement in the political regime: the political detainees have regained their freedom, and Minsk is ready for small confrontations with Moscow, still by refusing to recognize South Ossetia and Abazzia separated from Georgia, but Russia with which Belarus forms a paper confederation considers the two independent territories for a month.
'We have taken two steps, and one step backwards, but the most important thing is that the dialogue has been initiated between Belarus and the EU' - Alexander Milinkevich told our diary.
According to Opposite the 2006 presidential elections, it would be desirable for it to remain so, although "power has been feared in this parliament, which has no clout, of admitting even a single opponent".
The Opposition does not foresee any protest actions in the light of the results; rather, it tries to send its message about the need for economic reforms among voters.
'Of course we have a lot of worries on this side: we have to print our oppressive newspapers in Russia, the local authorities put obstacles in our wheels when it comes to broadcasting, but I can also see that they are increasingly supporting the elections, even in the circle of the elderly" - said Milinkevich, referring to the fact that in the current Lukashenko system, which regards its main result.
"Because of the indebtedness of the country and the increase in the trade deficit, the collapse of social security, 'evoking the silence of a cemetery', is only a matter of time" - Milinkevitch argues.
Contrary to the Opposition Politician, Russian observers described the elections as democratic.
Serguei Lebedev, leader of the Community of Self-Denual States, told the Russian news agency Ria Novosti: “People do not believe in words, but in deeds.” In this way, the absence of the opposition is only proof of the maturity of Belarusian voters.
