“The aim, therefore, is to restore to the worker the guarantee of insurance – he added – in such a way that the destility of an accident must not depend on spontaneity or the enlargement of the legislator.”
Point-to-point Mp3 reader, they put hearing at risk.
Listening full-volume music for an extended period using portable music devices such as MP3 readers, can cause permanent hearing injuries.
The European Commission, which, in a statement released in Brussels, mentions the opinion of the Scientific Committee on the unforeseen and recently identified health risks of the EU, published today.
'From this scientific report - let's say in the note - it shows that 5-10% of those for a period of at least five years listen to full-volume music using portable music for more than an hour a day per week are at risk of a permanent loss of auditory capacity."
'I'm concerned that such a large number of young people who frequently use mobile devices and full-volume mobile phones can unhappily unwittingly damage their hearing irreparably,' said Meglena Kuneva.
'According to the scientific data - continues the culprit - this is a clear risk and we need to react quickly.
What matters most is to raise the awareness of consumers and make this information in the public domain.
We also need to re-examine the existing control systems in view of scientific opinion, so that they are fully effective and can keep up with new technologies."
Brussels asked the Independent Scientific Committee to examine the problem, in view of the intensive use of portable musical devices and the increase in the number of young people exposed to the noise they generate.
'Scientists-completing the Ue executive confirm that there is good cause for concern, and that the European Commission is now discussing with the Member States and other interested parties, what measures could possibly be taken to better protect children and young people against exposure to noise from portable musical devices and similar devices.
There is already a European safety standard which limits the noise level of portable musical devices to 100Db, but there is a growing concern about hearing damage that could derive from excessive exposure to such noise sources.
It is possible to clearly limit these data using solutions such as the reduction of noise exposure levels and duration.
The Scientific Steering Committee points out the fact that users of portable appliances, who listen to full-volume music (more than 89 decibitels) for 5 hours per week, exceed the limits currently in force relating to the maximum noise level agreed in the workplace.
Those who listen to music in such conditions for prolonged periods are at risk of a permanent loss of auditory capacity after five years.
This involves 5-10% of users of these devices, and their number can be quantified at around 2.5 and 10 million people in the EU.
Alemanno gave the order to decelerate the Commission for the future of the capital.
Today we're starting, in March we're finishing, in June we're going to shoot them.
This is the Capitol timetable for the work of the 'Commission for the Future of Rome Capital', which took place this morning at the new sieve, behind the Baths of Caracalla.
Within six months, President Antonio Marzano and the other 49 components of the Commission will develop the guidelines for Rome.
In March, upon their completion, the Committee and the Communal Council will evaluate the analyses and projects, with a view to presenting in June, the Strategic Plan for Rome with the "Official Stamp" of Capitol and its Mayor Gianni Alemanno.
"The committee is absolutely independent; all the political, social and cultural ideas are represented - would like to stress Alemanno - it will have to put forward ideas for the development of Rome which can be used by the political decision-makers, who remain the Committee and the Communal Council.
No superposition of roles, but only the desire to have a corner to develop a great project for Rome.
By March, it will have finished its tasks and will then disappear, because this experiment must have a precise time duration.
"Juncio to Annunciation The Mayor - we will present the strategic plan resulting from the work carried out by the Commission for the Future of Rome Capital."
For those accusing the Commission of being useless, since its opinions will not be binding, Alemanno replied: "It all depends on the work that the Commission will be able to do, as well as on the quality of its proposals.
If the quality is there, as in France thanks to the Attali Commission, as in Rome, I am aware of it, its contribution will be fundamental.
Too often, politics is overwhelmed by the day-to-day management and emergencies that arise from time to time.
On the other hand, we want to move forward, and beyond, towards a deeper foresight, working seriously for the development of Rome and its future."
In addition, the mayor of Rome would like to point out that 'this Commission is not an unnecessary body or a bureaucratic package: it is absolutely gratuitous, and will not cost Roman dwellers a euro a euro, and will serve to study and involve the best intelligences that express the capital.
For Alemanno, in fact, "it is necessary to provide a strategic, neither firm nor immobile, in order to allow our city to improve, which it absolutely needs, while thinking of its future now a unitary spirit.
A future which, the Mayor of Rome states, 'will not be limited to the usual communal borders, but which will look any further' not only from the perspective of the future Metropolitan city and its legal status of Capital, but also in relation to the role that Rome can and should have, as the Capital of Europe and the Mediterranean".
