Usa, the economic debilitator is felt even among journalists: in the press, it is the time for deletions, and many accuse the Internet, this 'st murder' of newsprint.
Five hundred American journalists were questioned by the PEW Research Center about the status of their profession and mental control for the future.
Well, almost all journalists today are pessimistic about their future.
However, they did not openly declare to the information sterms, feeling less worried about credibility.
They don't feel lord as they used to.
The problem for American journalists is money.
The crisis of 2008 for these reporters is a crisis of destroyed economic models.
In addition, the deletions made in the newsroom, in the room where the writers are working on assembling newspapers, is a big problem.
It is a problem that must be solved, according to the journalists interviewed, if traditional journalism such as the general general public does not diminish significantly.
An unconspicuous but complex pessimism pervades American journalism.
Six out of ten senior executives and newspaper journalists are convinced that journalism at the national level is following the wrong path.
Locally, the executive group is 65%.
Published printed against broadcasting.
Journalists don't have a problem seeing where the web goes.
Biggest publishing houses are making a transition to the Internet, perceived by many as "the assassin" of newsprint.
The "economation" felt by journalists is increased by up to 79%.
The 500 interviewees admitted to changing the technology hoping that it "makes something good" for their journalism.
Nowadays, the greatest increase in U.S. Journalism lies between those who run the newsrooms – that is, the directors – and the reporters who work for them.
56 per cent of managers at the national level are convinced that reporters share their values, while at the local level this percentage falls to 23 per cent.
For some, this is related to 'reliability of work'.
Managers who expect to lose their jobs within the 3-year period are 47 per cent at the national level.
As far as journalists are concerned, let's do 30 per cent.
“Safe work” at staff level (our editors) in America is still on the agenda.
Only 13 per cent of journalists work under contract.
In a huge nation such as the Usa, with 300 million inhabitants and thousands of journalists, magazines, radio, television and the Internet, employers often offer whatever they want, and this quickly becomes a question of taking or letting go.
Directors almost always receive high salaries, while the editor can earn 10 thousand dollars a month, in addition to retirement and insurance payments.
As for vacations, the logic becomes complicated: x days after the first year, x days after the third, up to a total of 15 days, spent a certain number of years.
But their revelations indicate that American journalists are often “swrithing” during the year.
It is the fault of the climate.
