Elites in Danger.
The phenomenon had already manifested itself a few days a few days ago, with the vote in the House of Representatives, in contravention of the White-Municipal bail-sur-populated 700-dollar bail-up plan: a significant number of Americans were, and still is, more interested in punishing Wall Street than in rescuing the United States.
And it is the New York Times of yesterday that confirms to us that the current crisis divides the Republican Party, even more intertwined between its moderate East-Wor all-in-time East-Counsel, supported by federal institutions as well as business community, and its new soul, on the other hand, a conservative, hostile to "Washington" and the world of finance, powerful especially in Central and Southern states.
Of course, the faltering, which made itself felt despotically with the vote that I was talking about at the very beginning.
But what has just been mentioned is just one example of the changes, of the big changes that the current economic earthquake is prenouncing or already letting run: not only in the United States, but throughout the United States, and can be not just here.
In the United States as well as in Europe, the crisis seems to be speeding up the phenomena incubated for some time, to such an extent that, in the new climate, it solidifies and turns out.
The first of these phenomena is the recalculation, an extraordinary revival, of the two-category of a sovereign State, compared with a kind of renationalization of the economy.
The crisis, in fact, is a crisis of banking-financial institutions which, of course, have close relations with the foreign sector, but which, above all, are involved on a large-scale scale the balance sheets of individuals and families who live in a single country in a single State.
This immediately creates a political problem for which it governs it: i.e. to respond to the difficulties and protests of individuals and families who are also an electorate.
In a nutshell, the crisis appears to be an economically global, but political one, it is almost national.
Political internationalisation really seems out of the game or does not have much to say: the clear proof is furnished for us by the European Union which, as usual, between the various interests and between the various national strategies, cannot decide on any common political line.
In this way, it is from the government of every State that everyone is waiting for interventions, rescue and recovery plans, new rules, and, above all, an gentlery of appropriations: when we go through a difficult period, there are only the states that possess the economic resources, the mass of financial resources capable of trying to put things in place.
They also have the necessary resources and legitimisation to use them: two resources of inestimable value, elements that no international market or organization can ever have in a similar manner.
Moreover, this new emphasis of the nation-state crisis is in perfect harmony with the ever-major importance that the more recent economic developments in the economy tend to attribute to a factor quite linked to this element: territoriality.
Paradoxically, when we were convinced that we have now entered the kingdom of the network, of the increasingly sophisticated script, of non-patenting, convinced that globalized finance was now intended to dominate the world, we realized at a time of one, that our future will, on the contrary, will have to settle its scores more and more with rather unrealized things such as water, crops and oil.
Things that can certainly be transported from one place of the land to another, but which are absolutely related to a circumscriminated space, to a territory.
Thus, whoever is sovereign, certainly possesses more cards than the one who is not sovereign, i.e. one who has the misfortune to live in a place unharviled, without oil and without water.
Let me now arrive at the second novelty which, however, envisages a fracture.
Whoever says Territory, State, Government, inevitably says Politics, and therefore Leadership.
In this regard, the economic crisis seems to produce two converging phenomena.
On the one hand, an awareness of the objective need for authoritarian leadership, the search for a person who knows how to take the situation into its own hands.
On the other side, a wave of disrepute for existing leadership, an economic species that have proved to be inadequate and full of grey areas.
In addition, something that is even deeper, in a certain sense, even worrisome: disrepute disrepellent, an inconvenience image, a feeling of remoteness that tends to involve the whole class of directors in an increasing number of countries of the West.
Thus, the conviction of the various classes increases even more, before the actions are the equal ideas of social elites until now at their peak, their way of listening and of being, their culture in the complex acceptance of the term, to have the same time, and to be always more unrelated to the opinions of major majorities.
In a nutshell, the demand for leadership, feeds underground, a powerful and ramified movement of delegitimates of the ruling classes and traditional political orders, manifested themselves in improvised electoral revolts or, in the changes of collective humours of the latter laterally, from the conservative ascension in Bavaria, to the fall of Labour in Great United Kingdom.
One has the impression that the traditional elites, their parties, their programmes, but also their rites, their diaries, their enthusibilities, their format, would say to someone, are always more and more difficult, which does not date today, to comprehend the deep strata of Western societies, and that the economic crisis is potentially increasing and agitating in a disproportionate manner.
In view of all of this, talking about a current 'rebellion of the masses' on the agenda seems frankly exaggerated.
But it's clear, keeping our eyes wide open, it's not without reason.
The adhesives to walk on the walls.
Defeating the laws of gravity through a super glue and, in the future, walking on walls like Spiderman.
This is the objective of various American research centres, and today a team of four institutes is almost successful.
With an adhesive measuring barely 2.5 square centimetres, the researchers were able to hold a 100-kilogram body bonded to a vertical wall.
THE ETUDE - The American journal Science published the discoveries of selected researchers from the University of Dayton, Akron, the Georgia Institute of Technology and the Air Force Research Laboratory.
Hardware, invented by the team of researchers, is an evolution of those that we know that use a particular structure of carbon, called carbon nanotubes.
"The glue principle can be imagined as a series of spaghetti tubes."
Scientists were inspired by gecko paws, coated with microscopic bristles, which make it possible to provide the animal with a perfect adherent.
The tests carried out on various materials, such as glass, Teflon, and glass paper, have demonstrated that the new adhesive is ten times more powerful than the paws of these reptiles, and at the same time that it 'feels easily from the walnut,' explains Liming Dai, researcher at Dayton University's engineering faculty.
MARKET TO WORKER? – “This material could be used as a temporary adhesive” adds Liming Dai.
Maybe to walk on the walls like Spiderman? Maybe yes, but the researchers are thinking of much less fascinating usage such as the connection between electronic devices, since carbon nanotubes are conductive of heat and electric current.
Other applications could relate to Aerospace Engineering.
For the time being, no superhero.
RESEARCH – The University of Berkeley in California is also dedicated to seeking a new adhesive adhesive.
In an article in the American journal American Chemical Society, Californian researchers presented an adhesive consisting of microfibers such as Hile, which self-cleanses du du du dust over an area to ensure perfect and constant adherence.
Medications: Never with fruit juice.
Look out for grapefruit, Orange and Appellant Juice.
These beverages, consumed especially in the summer because which are thirsty and rich in vitamins, if combined with certain vital drugs, alter the so-integration which amplifies or decrease, depending on the case, their effectiveness, with even serious consequences for health.
David Nailey, Professor at the University of Western Ontario in London, reminds us of the risks of fruit juices whether inboxes, if associated with ingesting certain drugs; during the 236th American Chemical Society National Meeting in Philadelphia, he launches a new alarm: it is enough for a glass of grapefruit juice, orange and apples, to reduce their life-savvy juice.
Grapefruit juice has already been strongly criticised because it was held responsible for increasing the effects of certain anti-torhythmic, antibiotic, anxiolytic, antistamin and neurological drugs to toxic levels.
UNITED STATES – It was the Italian Medicines Agency itself, which gave the news in a two-year-old information bulletin, alerting doctors and patients not to under-assess the danger arising from this mix and to pay attention to what one is drinking if one takes medicines, such as Diazepam or Méthadone.
